CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters mandated by the Defense Acquisition Program Act and the matters necessary for the enforcement of the mandated matters.

Article 2 (Classification of weapons systems) Weapons systems defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 3 of the Defense Acquisition Program Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") shall be as follows: <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; Feb. 29, 2016; May 11, 2021>
  1. Weapons systems for command and control, and communications, such as communications networks;
  2. Weapons systems for surveillance and reconnaissance, such as radar;
  3. Mobile weapons systems, such as tanks and armored personnel carriers;
  4. Naval weapons systems, such as combat ships;
  5. Air weapons systems, such as combat aircraft;
  6. Firepower weapons systems, such as self-propelled artillery;
  7. Defense weapons systems, such as guided anti-air weaponry;
  8. Cyber weapons systems, such as the cyber battle management system;
  9. Space weapons systems, such as satellites;
  10. Other weapons systems, such as software for simulation analysis and simulation exercise and equipment essential for supporting combat forces.

Article 2-2 (Types of force support systems) Force support systems under subparagraph 4 of Article 3 of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Combat support equipment (including repair parts), such as general vehicles, special vehicles, power supply, power units, surveillance support equipment, maintenance equipment, ammunition and guided missile equipment, general equipment for combat support, measuring apparatus, telecommunications electronic equipment and service support equipment;
  2. Combat support materials, such as bulletproof materials, clothing, accouterments, foodstuffs, chemicals, oil, special textile materials, ammunition and guided missile supplies, electrical and electronic materials, service support materials, printing materials, etc.;
  3. Medical support materials such as medical equipment and medical supplies;
  4. Goods for education and training such as equipment, materials and ammunitions for education and training;
  5. Defense information systems, such as the resource management information system, defense modeling and simulation systems, and basic operating environments.
  6. Other force support systems, such as military facilities.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 1, 2020]

Article 2-3 (Scope of defense acquisition program contracts) (1) Goods prescribed by Presidential Decree in subparagraph 15d of Article 3 of the Act means the following goods:
  1. Goods which require urgent procurement for recovery from damage or prevention because any of the following grounds has caused or are expected to cause any damage to the lives, bodies, and property of citizens:
  a. Wartime, disturbance, incident, infiltration by the enemy, infiltration of the Republic of Korea, or emergency situation equivalent thereto;
  b. Terrorism defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on Counter-Terrorism for the Protection of Citizens and Public Security;
  c. Occurrence of a disaster, calamity, infectious disease, or other situations corresponding thereto;
  2. Goods necessary for promptly performing emergency affairs related to overseas dispatch.
  (2) Goods prescribed by Presidential Decree in subparagraph 15e of Article 3 of the Act means goods requiring excellent performance and quality to protect the lives and safety of soldiers among force support systems.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 2-4 (Definition of long-term contract) Contracts prescribed by Presidential Decree in subparagraph 16 of Article 3 of the Act means defense procurement contracts concluded in any of the following cases:
  1. Where it is necessary for several years to perform the contract;
  2. Where it is deemed inefficient or difficult to conclude a contract to be terminated within the relevant fiscal year in consideration of a long-term procurement plan, long-term repetitive requirements, economic conditions, etc.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

CHAPTER II TRANSPARENCY AND SPECIALIZATION IN EXECUTION OF DEFENSE AQUISITION PROGRAMS

Article 3 (Implementation of real-name policy system) (1) The major policies through which the real-name policy system shall be implemented pursuant to Article 5(1) of the Act shall be subject to deliberation and coordination by the Defense Acquisition Program Promotion Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") pursuant to Article 9(2) of the Act.
  (2) Where the plans, reports, etc. on the major policies are formulated pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (1), the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall have the position, post, and names of the participants, and the details of opinions, plans, reports, etc. recorded and retained by relevant department. When they are corrected or modified in the course of a decision or execution, he or she shall have the grounds for such correction or modification, the relevant persons and the relevant details recorded.
  (3) Where the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration hold public hearings, seminars, meetings of the relevant persons, etc. pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall require the department in charge to record the date and time when they wear held, names of participants, details of remarks, voting, etc.

Article 4 (Submission and details of pledge of integrity) (1) "Committee prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 6(1)2c of the Act means the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee under Article 61-12(1) (hereinafter referred to as the "Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee"). <Added on Apr. 30, 2024; Jul. 16, 2024>
  (2) Where the Minister of National Defense and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration appoint or commission members of the Committee, etc. classified as follows, he or she shall require the members to submit a pledge of integrity: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024; Jul. 16, 2024>
  1. Where submission is required by the Minister of National Defense:
  a. The Committee;
  b. The Military force policy subcommittee under Article 15(1)1;
  c. Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee established in the Ministry of National Defense;
  2. Where submission is required by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration:
  a. The defense acquisition program planning and management subcommittee under Article 15(1)2;
  b. The working committees under Article 15-2;
  c. Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee established in the Ministry of Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (3) Where public officials designated by the Minister of National Defense belong to the department related to defense acquisition programs in the Ministry of National Defense by new appointment, promotion, advancement, transfer, or change of position, the Minister of National Defense shall require them to submit a pledge of integrity. <Added on Nov. 4, 2014; Apr. 30, 2024>
  (4) Where public officials belonging to the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, or any persons become executive officers or employees of the Agency for Defense Development established under the Act on the Agency for Defense Development (hereinafter referred to as the "Agency for Defense Development") or the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality established under Article 32 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Defense Agency for Technology and Quality"), by new appointment, promotion, advancement, transfer, or change of positions, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall require them to submit a pledge of integrity. <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; May 11, 2021; Apr. 30, 2024>
  (5) Where defense contractors, general enterprises (hereinafter referred to as "defense contractors, etc."), general enterprises which have no relationship to the defense industry, or research institutes specified in Article 6(1)4 of the Act are registered as bidders to participate in defense acquisition programs, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall require them to submit a pledge of integrity. <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Jul. 19, 2016; Apr. 30, 2024>
  (6) Where a military supply sales agency specified in Article 6(1)5 of the Act enters into a contract on intermediation or agency with a foreign enterprise participating in a defense acquisition program, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall require it to submit a pledge of integrity. <Added on Jul. 19, 2016; Apr. 30, 2024>
  (7) "Amounts of money prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 6(1)6 of the Act means one billion won, respectively. <Added on Sep. 22, 2017; Apr. 30, 2024>
  (8) Where a defense contractor, etc. which has concluded a defense procurement contract, general enterprise, specialized research institute, or general research institute which has not related to defense acquisition programs, intends to conclude a subcontract for a defense procurement contract with a contractor (referring to a supplier in cases of a sales contract; hereinafter referred to as "subcontractor") or where a subcontractor intends to conclude a sub-subcontract for a defense procurement contract, it shall notify the other party to the contract in advance of the fact that it is required to submit a pledge of integrity to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration pursuant to Article 6(1) of the Act. <Added on Sep. 22, 2017; Apr. 30, 2024>
  (9) Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree in Article 6(2)4 of the Act means matters concerning requesting or receiving any of the following information from relevant public officials and prohibiting any act of arbitrarily providing or divulging information to a third party: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024; Jul. 16, 2024>
  1. The following information on the implementation of defense acquisition programs:
  a. Verification of appropriateness of requirements for weapons systems, etc. under Article 13(3) of the Act;
  b. Project feasibility studies under Article 14-2 of the Act;
  c. Decision on requirements for weapons systems, etc. under Article 15 of the Act;
  d. Prior conceptual studies under Article 15-3 of the Act;
  e. Prior studies under the main clause of Article 17(1) of the Act;
  f. Analysis and evaluation of defense force improvement projects under Article 23 of the Act;
  g. Other information on promotion of defense acquisition programs equivalent to those referred to in items a through f;
  2. Information on major decision-making by committees, etc. under the following items:
  a. The Committee;
  b. Subcommittees and working committees under Article 10 of the Act;
  c. Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee;
  3. Information on major outcomes learned in the course of contract performance or derived as a result of contract performance.
  (10) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to allow a person falling under Article 6(1)4 or 5 of the Act to continuously perform the relevant tendering procedure or successful tender or contract pursuant to the proviso of Article 6(4) of the Act, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may undergo deliberation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee, if deemed necessary in consideration of the following matters, etc.: <Added on Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Characteristics of subject matter of contract;
  2. Seriousness of misconduct;
  3. The degree of performance and the period of performance of the relevant contract;
  4. The urgency of the performance of the contract;
  5. The amount of loss incurred by the State due to the suspension of performance of a contract.

Article 5 (Formation and term of office of ombudsmen) (1) The ombudsmen under Article 6(5) of the Act shall consist of up to 3 persons commissioned by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010; Apr. 30, 2024; Dec. 23, 2025>
  (2) Each ombudsman shall hold office for a term of 2 years and may be appointed consecutively for only 1 further term. <Amended on Dec. 23, 2025>
  (3) An ombudsman commissioned under paragraph (1) shall not be dismissed against his or her will; provided, where an ombudsman falls under any of subparagraphs 2 through 4, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may dismiss him or her, and where he or she falls under subparagraph 1, 5, or 6, the Administrator shall dismiss him or her: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010; Apr. 30, 2024; Dec. 23, 2025>
  1. Where he or she has given or received money or other articles or entertainment in connection with his or her duties;
  2. Where he or she has been negligent in performing his or her duties due to irresponsibility, or intentionally evaded the execution of duties;
  3. Where he or she has performed an act in violation of security in the course of performing his or her duties;
  4. Where he or she has violated the proviso of Article 7(5);
  5. Where it is recognized as being difficult for him or her to execute his or her duties as usual due to physical or mental incapacity;
  6. Where an ombudsman is commissioned as an ombudsman, after being commissioned as an ombudsman, the grounds prescribed in the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 6(6) of the Act are clarified or where the ombudsman concurrently holds a position prescribed in the subparagraphs of Article 6(9) of the Act.
  [Title Amended on Oct. 1, 2010]

Article 6 (Representative ombudsman) (1) For the purposes of efficient operation of the ombudsman system, there may be a representative ombudsman.
  (2) The representative ombudsman shall be elected from among the ombudsmen.
  (3) The representative ombudsman shall represent other ombudsmen, and take overall control over the affairs of the ombudsmen.
  (4) Where the representative ombudsman fails to perform his or her duties temporarily, the ombudsman whom the representative ombudsman has appointed in advance shall perform his or her duties as proxy.
  (5) The representative ombudsman shall submit details of the semi-annual accomplishments of the ombudsmen to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration within one month after the end of each half-year.

Article 7 (Procedures for handling investigation of civil petitions) (1) When an ombudsman makes a request for correction or inspection, etc. pursuant to the main clause of Article 6(7) of the Act, with the exception of its subparagraphs, the representative ombudsman shall reach an agreement between all ombudsmen and the representative ombudsman. <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  (2) Where the results of an investigation on a civil petition does not require a request for correction or inspection, but where the improvement of institutions, policies, etc. is deemed necessary, ombudsmen may advise the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration to make a reasonable improvement therein or express their opinions to him or her.
  (3) Where an ombudsman receives an original copy of the relevant documents in the course of an investigation pursuant to Article 6(8) of the Act, he or she shall return it within 7 days from the date of receipt thereof. <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  (4) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration receives a request for correction or inspection or such, from the representative ombudsman pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the representative ombudsman of the result of such management within 30 days from the date he or she receives a request for correction or inspection, etc.
  (5) The representative ombudsman may publicize the details of a request for correction or inspection, etc. referred to in paragraph (1) and the details of the result of management referred to in paragraph (4); provided, this shall not apply to information subject to non-disclosure pursuant to Article 9 of the Official Information Disclosure Act.
  (6) For the efficient activities of ombudsmen, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may subsidize allowances, travel expenses and other necessary expenses and provide them with an office, etc. within the budget
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Oct. 1, 2010]

Article 8 (Inquiry into criminal records) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration requests criminal records inquiry pursuant to Article 6-2(1) of the Act, he or she shall submit a written request for criminal records inquiry prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense to the Commissioner General of the National Police Agency, the chief prosecutor of a district prosecutors' office (including the chief of a branch office; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) or the head of the Military Prosecutors' Office. In such cases, a written consent of a person subject to criminal records inquiry prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense shall be attached thereto.
  (2) Upon receipt of a request for inquiry into criminal records pursuant to Article 6-2(1) of the Act and paragraph (1) of this Article, the Commissioner General of the National Police Agency, chief prosecutors of district prosecutors' offices, and heads of military prosecutors' offices shall reply to the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration in accordance with a written reply to inquiry into criminal records prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense after verifying the criminal records of a person subject to inquiry into criminal records for the recent five years from the date of such request, which constitute a violation of the Military Secret Protection Act or the Defense Technology Protection Act.
  (3) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may request the relevant defense contractor, etc., general enterprise, or research institute not related to the defense industry, to provide information on persons subject to inquiries about criminal records, which is necessary to request inquiries about criminal records pursuant to Article 6-2(1) of the Act and paragraph (1) of this Article.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 16, 2024]

Article 9 Deleted. <Oct. 1, 2010>

Article 10 Deleted. <Oct. 20, 2008>

Article 11 (Scope of key positions and standards for qualification) (1) The positions particularly deemed to require expertise referred to in Article 7(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "key position") shall be positions such as director general or director of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration and the agencies under its control, and the head of the integrated project administration team.
  (2) Persons eligible for key positions pursuant to Article 7 of the Act shall satisfy any of the following qualifications; provided, a person for whom five years have not elapsed since he or she was subject to a heavy disciplinary measure or sentenced to imprisonment without labor or heavier punishment pursuant to Article 57(1) of the Military Personnel Management Act or Article 1-3 of the Decree on Disciplinary Action against Public Officials shall not be appointed to any key position: <Amended on Mar. 18, 2009; Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Persons who have worked for three or more years in a field relating to the positions to be appointed;
  2. Persons who hold qualifications or a bachelor's or higher degree in a field relating to a defense acquisition program;
  3. Persons who have been educated in a field relating to a defense acquisition program.
  (3) Matters necessary regarding the extent of the fields relating to the positions to be appointed, qualifications, degrees, kinds of education, etc. under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.

Article 12 (Research of legal issues regarding defense acquisition programs) Matters that require prior legal review in entering into a contract or negotiation, etc. pursuant to Article 8 of the Act shall be as listed in the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010; Mar. 30, 2021>
  1. Matters relating to the basic strategy for the promotion of projects for improving defense capability, the total cost of which is 10 billion won or more;
  2. Matters relating to the preparation of requests for project proposals regarding defense acquisition programs and the evaluation thereof;
  3. All kinds of contracts and agreements regarding defense acquisition programs;
  4. Matters regarding major decision-making on projects for improving defense capability prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration;
  5. Matters regarding the formulation of basic plans for defense science and technology innovation under Article 13(3) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  6. Matters regarding the revocation of designation of defense contractors (hereinafter referred to as "defense contractors"), defense materials (hereinafter referred to as "defense materials") or guarantee organizations under Article 48 of the Act;
  7. Matters regarding the termination of commission of specialized research institutes under Article 63;
  8. Other matters that the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration recognizes as necessary for the smooth implementation of defense acquisition programs.

Article 12-2 (Operation of in-house supervisory organization) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may establish and operate an in-house supervisory organization, as prescribed by related statutes or regulations, such as the Decree on the Organization of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration and the agencies under its jurisdiction, for the inspection and investigation of all the defense acquisition programs, the collection of information related to defense acquisition programs, and the prevention of absurdities and irregularities, and legal reviews, etc. on matters under Article 12.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall guarantee independence at the maximum in performing duties of an in-house supervisory organization under paragraph (1) in order to ensure transparency in defense acquisition programs.
  (3) Matters necessary for the operation, etc. of an in-house supervisory organization under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 31, 2016]

Article 13 (Composition of Commission) (1) The members of the Committee referred to in Article 9(4)2 of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Dec. 7, 2010; Dec. 17, 2013; Nov. 4, 2014; Jul. 25, 2023; Feb. 19, 2026>
  1. The Deputy Minister of National Defense;
  2. The Vice Administrator, the Director General of the Current Capabilities Program Agency, and the Director General of the Advanced Capabilities Program Agency of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration;
  3. The Director General of Strategy and Planning of the Headquarters of the Joint Chiefs of Staff;
  4. The Deputy Chiefs of Staff of the Army, Navy, and Air Force (hereinafter referred to as "each service branch") and the Assistant Commandant of the Marine Corps.
  (2) The members commissioned under Article 9(4)5 and 6 of the Act shall hold office for a term of 2 years and may be appointed for only 1 further term. ＜ Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Dec. 23, 2025 ＞
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Oct. 1, 2010]

Article 14 (Operation of Commission) (1) The Chairperson of the Committee (hereinafter referred to as "Chairperson") shall represent the Committee and exercise an overall control over its business.
  (2) Where the Chairperson fails to perform his or her duties due to unavoidable reasons, the Vice Chairperson shall perform the duties as proxy.
  (3) The Committee shall convene a meeting when at least one third of the members on the register makes a request, or when the Chairperson or Vice Chairperson deems it as necessary.
  (4) Meetings of the Committee shall be held with a majority of the members on the register, and pass resolutions upon approval by a majority of those members present, and the vote shall be made by an open vote.
  (5) Deleted. <Dec. 17, 2013>
  (6) Where it is necessary for the deliberation upon and coordination of projects regarding the National Intelligence Service, the Committee may have a public official belonging to the National Intelligence Service attend a meeting and hear his or her opinions. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010>
  (7) Other matters necessary for the operation of the Committee shall be prescribed by the Chairperson after the resolution by the Committee. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010>

Article 14-2 (Exclusion, challenge, and abstention of members) (1) Where a member of the Committee falls under any of the following, he or she shall be excluded from deliberation or coordination of the relevant item on the agenda:
  1. Where he or she or a corporation or organization to which he or she belongs has an interest in the relevant item on the agenda;
  2. Where a person who is his or her spouse, his or her relative within the fourth degree of consanguinity or his or her relative by marriage within the second degree of consanguinity has an interest in the relevant item on the agenda;
  3. Where he or she is deemed to have a direct interest in the resolution of the relevant item on the agenda.
  (2) Where a person who has a direct interest in matters deliberated or coordinated by the Committee has a ground to hardly expect a member of the Committee to fairly perform his or her duties, such person may file an application for challenge of such member with the Committee, and where the Committee deems such application for challenge reasonable, it shall decide the challenge by adopting a resolution. In such cases, no member subject to application for challenge shall participate in the resolution.
  (3) Where a member of the Committee has a ground referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), he or she shall inform the Committee of the fact that he or she has such ground, and avoid deliberating on the relevant item on the agenda.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 20, 2017]

Article 14-3 (Dismissal of members) Where a commissioned member of the Committee falls under any of the following, the Minister of National Defense may dismiss the relevant member of the Committee:
  1. Where he or she becomes unable to perform the duties due to a mental or physical disability;
  2. Where he or she engages in any misconduct in connection with the duties;
  3. Where he or she is deemed unsuitable as a member due to neglect of a duty, loss of dignity, or any other reason;
  4. Where he or she fails to refrain himself or herself despite that he or she falls under a case set forth in any of the subparagraphs of Article 14-2(1);
  5. Where he or she voluntarily admits that it is difficult to perform the duties.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 20, 2017]

Article 15 (Subcommittees) (1) Matters subject to deliberation and coordination by subcommittees by field and each subcommittee under Article 10 of the Act shall be as follows; provided, matters deliberated on by the Working Committee for Space Development of National Security under the proviso of Article 6(7) of the Space Development Promotion Act shall be excluded herefrom: <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Sep. 24, 2019; Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Military force policy subcommittee: Matters prescribed in Article 9(2)2, 10, and 14 of the Act;
  2. Defense acquisition program planning and management subcommittee: Matters prescribed in Article 9(2)1, 3 through 9, and 11 through 14 of the Act;
  3. Deleted; <Sep. 24, 2019>
  4. Deleted. <Sep. 24, 2019>
  (2) Each subcommittee shall be comprised of not less than 5 nor more than 26 members including one chairperson, in consideration of gender. <Amended on Jun. 20, 2017; Sep. 24, 2019>
  (3) The chairperson of each subcommittee shall be appointed by the Chairperson on the recommendation of the Vice Chairperson, from among the members of the Committee, and the members of each subcommittee shall be appointed or commissioned by the Chairperson on the recommendation of the Vice Chairperson, from among the following persons: <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; Apr. 14, 2015; Jun. 20, 2017>
  1. Persons recommended respectively by the members of the Committee under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of National Defense, the Headquarters of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, the headquarters of each service branch, and the Headquarters of the Marine Corps, from among public officials at the level of director general or military officers of the rank of general in the relevant agencies;
  2. Public officials at the level of director general or military officers of the rank of general belonging to the Defense Acquisition Program Administration;
  3. Persons recommended by the members of the Committee falling under Article 9(4)3 and 4 of the Act, from among public officials at the level of director general or executive officers or employees of the agencies to which such members belong;
  4. Persons commissioned pursuant to Article 9(4)5 and 6 of the Act.
  (4) The term of office for members of each subcommittee commissioned pursuant to paragraph (3)1 through 3 shall coincide with the term of service in their positions, and the term of office for members of each subcommittee appointed pursuant to subparagraph 4 of that paragraph shall coincide with the term of service as a member of the Committee.
  (5) Article 14(1) through (4) and (7), 14-2 and 14-3 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation of subcommittees. In such cases, the "Committee" shall be construed as "each subcommittee", the "Chairperson" as the "chairperson of each subcommittee", the "Vice Chairperson" as a "member designated in advance by the chairperson of each subcommittee." <Amended on Jun. 20, 2017>

Article 15-2 (Working committees) (1) Working committees necessary in each field shall be established under the control of subcommittees (excluding the military power policy subcommittee) prescribed in Article 15(1) pursuant to Article 10(3) of the Act.
  (2) Each working committee shall deliberate on and coordinate matters requiring prior review before deliberation or coordination by the relevant subcommittee and other matters necessary for supporting affairs of the subcommittee.
  (3) Each working committee shall be comprised of not less than 5 nor more than 20 members, including one chairperson, in consideration of gender.
  (4) A person appointed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, from among public officials at the level of director or military officers of the rank of general of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, shall serve as the chairperson of each working committee, and the following persons shall serve as members of each working committee:
  1. Persons appointed by the head of an agency to which they belong from among public officials or military officers of the rank of section chief belonging to the Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, the Headquarters of each service branch and Marine Corps;
  2. Persons appointed by the head of the relevant agency from among public officials of the rank of section chief or higher in agencies to which members of the Committee prescribed in Article 9(4)3 of the Act belong;
  3. Persons recommended by members of the Committee prescribed in Article 9(4)4 of the Act from among executive officers and employees under their jurisdiction;
  4. Persons commissioned by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, who are experts who have expert knowledge of and experience in defense acquisition programs.
  (5) Each working committee shall convene a meeting, when the chairperson of each working committee deems it necessary.
  (6) Article 14(1), (2) and (4), 14-2, and 14-3 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation of each working committee. In such cases, "Committee" shall be construed as "each working committee;" "Chairperson" as "chairperson of each working committee;" "Minister of National Defense" as "Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration;" and "Vice Chairperson" as "a member designated in advance by the chairperson of each working committee," respectively.
  (7) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (6), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall prescribe matters necessary for the organization, operation, etc. of each working committee.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 20, 2017]

Article 16 (Technical experts) (1) The number of expert members referred to in Article 10(4) of the Act shall be not less than two nor more than five, and shall be commissioned by the Chairperson on the recommendation of the Vice Chairperson. <Amended on Jun. 20, 2017>
  (2) The term of office of expert members referred to in paragraph (1) shall be one year, and they may be reappointed; provided, where an expert member is entrusted for consultation on a specific matter, he or she shall be deemed discharged from such commission on the completion of such consultation.

Article 17 (Requests for submission of data) (1) Where necessary for deliberation and coordination, the Committee, a subcommittee or working committee may request the Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, armed forces, the Agency for Defense Development, the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, defense contractors, etc. to submit data or present its opinions. ＜ Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Jun. 20, 2017>
  (2) Each agency or organization, in receipt of a request to submit data or present opinions pursuant to paragraph (1), shall cooperate therewith, in the absence of good cause.

Article 18 (Allowances and travel expenses) The members present at the Committee, a subcommittee and working committee, expert members, and persons who have attended and submitted opinions to the Committee, a subcommittee and working committee pursuant to the provisions of Article 17 may be paid allowances and travel expenses within the budget; provided, where the relevant public official has attended the Committee in connection with his or her duties, this shall not apply. <Amended on Jun. 20, 2017>

CHAPTER III DEFENSE FORCE IMPROVEMENT PROJECTS

SECTION 1 Principles for Execution of Defense Force Improvement Projects

Article 19 (Request for manpower support) Where manpower support from the relevant organizations, such as each service branch, agencies under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense (including military units under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense; hereinafter the same shall apply), the Agency for Defense Development, and the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, is necessary for the efficient operation of the comprehensive project administration system pursuant to Article 12 of the Act in executing defense force improvement projects, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may request the relevant agencies and organizations to provide manpower support, following deliberation by the Committee. In such cases, where manpower support from each service branch and the agencies under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense is necessary, he or she shall request the Minister of National Defense to provide manpower support.

SECTION 2 Medium-Term National Defense Plan and Budget

Article 20 (Formulation of medium-term national defense plans) (1) In order to formulate a medium-term national defense plan pursuant to Article 13(1) of the Act, the Minister of National Defense shall prepare guidelines for the formulation of medium-term national defense plans (hereinafter referred to as "guidelines for medium-term national defense plans").
  (2) Where the Minister of National Defense intends to prepare the guidelines for medium-term national defense plans pursuant to paragraph (1), with regard to defense force improvement projects, he or she may receive a prior written opinion from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, and reflect it in such guidelines.
  (3) Where the Minister of National Defense formulates the guidelines for medium-term national defense plans or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration prepares a written opinion pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall consider the following: <Amended on Jan. 22, 2019; Mar. 30, 2021>
  1. Matters provided for in Article 22(4)1 through 5;
  2. The order of priorities in the requirements for weapons systems for the purposes of achieving goals regarding combined military strategy;
  3. Master plans for defense science and technology innovation under Article 6 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act.
  (4) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to formulate a written request for a medium-term plan for defense force improvement projects pursuant to Article 13(2) of the Act, the requirements for support elements of force integration and pilot projects pursuant to Article 17-2(1) of the Act shall be submitted to such Minister by the headquarters of each service branch, the Headquarters of the Marine Corps, and agencies under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; Nov. 4, 2014; Aug. 16, 2023; Apr. 30, 2024>

Article 20-2 (Requirement reviews) (1) A review of the adequacy of requirements for weapons systems, etc. under Article 13(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "requirement review") shall be performed with respect to the requirements deemed necessary by the Minister of National Defense for a medium-term national defense plan. <Amended on Dec. 23, 2025>
  (2) Requirement reviews shall be deliberated on by the Requirement Review Committee under Article 20-3.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraph (1) and (2), matters necessary for the methods, procedures, etc. of requirement reviews shall be prescribed by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Nov. 4, 2014]

Article 20-3 (Requirement Review Committee) (1) The Minister of National Defense may establish and operate a review committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Review Committee") under Article 20-2(2), where necessary to deliberate on requirement reviews. <Amended on Oct. 4, 2022>
  (2) The Review Committee shall consist of up to 15 members, including 1 chairperson. <Amended on Dec. 23, 2025>
  (3) The Director General of the Military Force Policy Bureau of the Ministry of National Defense shall serve as the chairperson of the Review Committee; members of the Committee shall be persons specified in subparagraphs 1 through 3; and, if necessary, persons specified in subparagraph 4 or 5 may also be included as members: <Amended on Dec. 23, 2025>
  1. 1 person designated by the head of each relevant agency from among military personnel or civilian military employees at the division chief level in a relevant department of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, the headquarters of each service branch, or the Marine Corps Command;
  2. A person designated by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration from among public officials or military personnel at the division chief level in a relevant department of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration;
  3. 1 person designated by the head of each relevant institution from among executive officers and employees of the Korea Institute for Defense Analyses, the Agency for Defense Development, or the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality;
  4. A person commissioned by the Minister of National Defense from among executive officers and employees of a government-funded research institute established under the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes, upon recommendation by the head of the relevant institute;
  5. A person commissioned by the Minister of National Defense from among persons with extensive expertise and experience in requirements for weapons systems, etc., defense acquisition programs, or economic and industrial fields.
  (4) The Minister of National Defense may dissolve the Review Committee, if the Minister recognizes that the purpose of organization of the Review Committee has been accomplished. <Added on Oct. 4, 2022>
  (5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for the operation of the Review Committee shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on Oct. 4, 2022; Dec. 23, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Nov. 4, 2014]

Article 21 (Budget formulation) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense prepares guidelines for budget formulation referred to in Article 14(1) of the Act with regard to defense force improvement projects, he or she shall receive, in advance, written opinions from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration and reflect them in such guidelines.
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration formulates a budget for defense force improvement projects, he or she shall receive data required for budgeting support elements of force integration and pilot projects pursuant to Article 17-2(1) of the Act from the headquarters of each service branch, the Headquarters of the Marine Corps, and agencies under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; Aug. 16, 2023; Apr. 30, 2024>

Article 21-2 (Project feasibility studies) "New large projects prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 14-2(1) of the Act means the following new projects: <Amended on Sep. 8, 2026>
  1. Projects under Article 14-2(1)1 and 3 of the Act (excluding those under subparagraph 5a of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act): A total project cost of at least 50 billion won;
  2. Projects under Article 14-2(1)2 and 3 of the Act (limited to those under subparagraph 5a of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act): A total project cost of at least 100 billion won.
  [This Article Added on May 3, 2022]

SECTION 3 Decision on Requirements and Revision Thereof

Article 22 (Decision-making processes regarding requirements) (1) Where the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff intends to determine the requirements for weapons systems, etc. pursuant to Article 15(1) of the Act, he or she shall first obtain written proposals of requirements, including the following, from the Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, the headquarters of each service branch, the Headquarters of the Marine Corps, and agencies under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense (hereinafter referred to as "agencies proposing requirements"); in such cases, where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration decides to pursue a counter-purchase under Article 15(1)1 of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act and Article 20(2) of the Enforcement Decree of that Act, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff may obtain a written proposal of requirements reflecting the requirements for the weapons systems to be purchased, to the extent that doing so does not interfere with the timing of fielding, etc. of the weapons systems: <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Jan. 22, 2019; Feb. 11, 2022; Apr. 30, 2024; May 19, 2026>
  1. Necessity, operational concept, time of force integration, and required quantities of weapons systems, etc.;
  2. Required operational capability (referring to the performance of weapons systems, etc. necessary for operational employment; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  3. A plan to develop required operational capability in step with technical development trends (excluding cases where it is inappropriate or impracticable to devise such plan when taking into account the characteristics of weapons systems, project sizes, and other factors);
  4. Requirements for support elements of force integration;
  5. Other reference materials necessary to determine requirements, etc. for weapons systems.
  (2) Where it is necessary for each agency proposing requirements to propose objective and reasonable requirements before it submits a written proposal of requirements pursuant to paragraph (1), it may require a specialized research institute or a general research institute prescribed by the Minister of National Defense to perform research on the necessity, operational concept, and required operational capability of weapons systems, etc. <Added on Jan. 22, 2019>
  (3) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall review written proposals of requirements submitted by agencies proposing requirements pursuant to paragraph (1) and formulate a force requirement plan, including matters under the subparagraphs of that paragraph. <Amended on Jan. 22, 2019>
  (4) Each agency proposing requirements or the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall consider the following matters when preparing written proposals of requirements referred to in paragraph (1) or formulating force requirement plans referred to in paragraph (3): <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Jan. 22, 2019>
  1. The basic direction-setting for policies on national defense;
  2. Information acquired by analyzing the domestic and international situation of national defense;
  3. The basic direction-setting for implementing a plan formulated by the Joint Chiefs of Staff for the construction of military capabilities based on current conditions of national security, military strategies, etc.;
  4. The level of defense science and technology developed and secured;
  5. The appropriate working efficiency and production capacity of defense contractors, etc.;
  6. Matters regarding the maintenance and repair of weapons systems;
  7. Compatibility and interoperability.
  (5) The Agency for Defense Development, the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, defense contractors, etc., or research institutes specified in Article 6(1)4 of the Act may present their opinions on requirements to the heads of agencies proposing requirements and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff. <Amended on May 28, 2018; Jan. 22, 2019>
  (6) Where the Chiefs of Staff of each service branch and the Commander of the Marine Corps determine the requirements for weapons systems, etc. under the former part of Article 15(1) of the Act, they shall prepare a draft form requirements for force including the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), taking into consideration the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (4), and shall be determined through self-deliberation procedures prescribed by the Chiefs of Staff of each service branch and the Commander of the Marine Corps. If necessary to prepare a draft form requirements for force scientifically and quantitatively, opinions on the requirements of the agencies, etc. referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (7) may be heard. <Added on Jul. 16, 2024>
  (7) Where necessary for formulating force requirement plans referred to in paragraph (3) of this Article in a scientific and quantitative manner prescribed in the latter part of Article 15(1) of the Act, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall hear opinions on requirements of the following agencies, etc. or operate a joint concept team comprised of public officials or employees of the agencies prescribed in subparagraphs 1 and 2 and experts prescribed in subparagraph 4. In such cases, matters necessary for the organization and operation of the joint concept team shall be determined by the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff: <Amended on May 28, 2018; Jan. 22, 2019; Jul. 16, 2024>
  1. The Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, and the armed forces;
  2. The Korea Institute for Defense Analyses, the Agency for Defense Development, and the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality;
  3. Party interested in defense contractors, etc.;
  4. Civilian specialists in defense industry field.
  (8) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall hear the opinions of the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration on the force requirement plans referred to in paragraph (3) of this Article before deliberation by the meeting of the Joint Chiefs of Staff prescribed in the latter part of Article 15(1) of the Act. <Added on May 28, 2018; Jan. 22, 2019; Jul. 16, 2024>
  (9) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (8), matters necessary for the decision-making processes regarding requirements shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the National Defense. <Amended on May 28, 2018; Jan. 22, 2019; Jul. 16, 2024>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 17, 2013]

Article 22-2 (Procedures for decision on expeditious requirements) (1) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall, when he or she intends to determine expeditious requirement (hereinafter referred to as "expeditious requirement") for weapons systems to which the mature technology of the private sector is applied pursuant to Article 15-2(1) of the Act, receive a written proposal for expeditious requirement from the agencies proposing requirements, including the following matters: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Necessity, operational concept, time of force integration, and required quantities of weapons systems;
  2. Required operational capability;
  3. Requirements for support elements of force integration;
  4. Other reference materials to determine expeditious requirements, etc.
  (2) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall review the written proposal for expeditious requirement pursuant to paragraph (1) and shall prepare a force requirement plan, including matters under the subparagraphs of the aforesaid paragraph.
  (3) Each agency proposing requirements or the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall, when he or she prepares a written proposal for expeditious requirement referred to in paragraph (1) or a force requirement plan referred to in paragraph (2), take into account the matters in each subparagraph of Article 22(4).
  (4) The Agency for Defense Development, the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, defense contractors, etc., or research institutes specified in Article 6(1)4d and e of the Act may present their opinions on expeditious requirements to the heads of agencies proposing requirements and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.
  (5) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall, as necessary to scientifically and quantitatively prepare a force requirement plan under paragraph (2), hear opinion from the following agencies and others on expedited requirements or operate an integrated concept team consisting of public officials and employees from agencies under paragraphs (1) and (2) and civilian specialists under paragraph (4); in such cases, necessary matters for the composition and operation of the integrated concept team shall be decided by the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff:
  1. The Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, and the armed forces;
  2. The Korea Institute for Defense Analyses, the Agency for Defense Development, and the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality;
  3. Party interested in defense contractors, etc.;
  4. Civilian specialists in defense industry field.
  (6) Notwithstanding paragraph (5), the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff may, when he or she performs the prior conceptual studies under Article 15-3(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "prior conceptual studies"), simplify the organization and operation of the integrated concept team, such as comprising the integrated concept team only of public officials and employees belonging to the institutions under paragraph (5)1 and 2, as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense.
  (7) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall, under the latter part of Article 15-2(1) of the Act, consider the opinions of the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration before the deliberation of the Joint Chiefs of Staff on the written proposal for expeditious requirement referred to in paragraph (2) of this Article.
  (8) Except as otherwise provided in paragraphs (1) through (7), detailed procedures for determining expedited requirements shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 22-3 (Procedures for conducting prior conceptual studies) (1) Where the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff intends to require the staff of the Agency for Defense Development, etc. to carry out the prior conceptual studies under Article 15-3(1) of the Act, he or she shall receive a request for the prior conceptual studies from the agency proposing requirements.
  (2) The Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall, in receipt of a written request for prior conceptual studies pursuant to paragraph (1), select projects subject to the prior conceptual studies, in consideration of the necessity of research, urgency, the ability of an institution that performs the prior conceptual studies, such as the Agency for Defense Development (hereinafter referred to as "institutions performing prior conceptual studies"), and determine the order of performing prior conceptual studies. In such cases, the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff shall hear the opinions of the heads of agencies proposing requirements, the Commissioner of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, and institutions performing prior conceptual studies.
  (3) When institutions performing prior conceptual studies perform prior conceptual studies, they shall include the following in the scope of the studies: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Necessity and operational concept of weapons systems, etc.;
  2. Required operational capability;
  3. Requirements for support elements of force integration;
  4. Analysis of alternatives;
  5. Possibility of expeditious armed forces (limited to cases where prior conceptual studies are conducted to determine expeditious requirements);
  6. Examining whether it is possible to combine the developmental test and evaluation and operational test pursuant to the proviso of Article 21(3) of the Act (limited to cases where prior conceptual studies are conducted to determine expeditious requirements);
  7. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of National Defense in order to efficiently conduct prior conceptual studies.
  (4) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), matters necessary for procedures for conducting prior conceptual studies shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 23 (Revision to minor matters of requirements) (1) "Minor matter prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso of Article 16(2) of the Act means the following: <Amended on Feb. 11, 2022; Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Change of the annual quantity, or timing of fielding, of weapons systems, etc. on the grounds for changes in financial resources in the formulation of a medium-term national defense plan or formulation and execution of a budget pursuant to Article 13(1) and 14 of the Act;
  1-2. The revision of the following matters due to budgetary reasons in the course of formulating a basic strategy for implementing defense force improvement projects under Article 24(1) (limited to cases where the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff deems that the necessity and operational concept of weapons systems, etc. are not changed):
  a. Required quantities of weapons systems, etc. (including annual quantities);
  b. Time of force integration of weapons systems, etc.;
  c. Required operational capabilities of weapons systems, etc.;
  2. Change of technical and incidental performance regarding weapons systems, etc.;
  3. Revision of plan for securing support elements for force integration.
  (2) Where any matter specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) is revised by the Committee or subcommittee in the process of deliberating on or coordinating medium-term plans, budgets of defense force improvement projects, or basic strategies for implementing defense force improvement projects, etc. (limited to cases deliberated on or coordinated by the Committee in cases of revision to the matters provided in paragraph (1)1-2), requirements thereof shall be deemed revised. <Amended on Feb. 11, 2022>

SECTION 4 Execution of Defense Force Improvement Projects

Article 24 (Methods of implementing defense force improvement projects) (1) Where requirements are determined under Article 15(1) or 15-2(1) of the Act, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall formulate a basic strategy for promoting defense force improvement projects and refer the strategy to the Committee for deliberation. <Amended on Aug. 16, 2023; Jun. 30, 2026>
  (2) The basic strategy for project implementation under paragraph (1) shall include the following: <Amended on Jan. 22, 2019; Aug. 16, 2023>
  1. Details of review on research and development, or decisions on purchasing;
  2. Matters regarding the types of research and development, methods of purchasing, etc.;
  3. Detailed direction-setting for project implementation, following research and development, or purchasing;
  4. Methods for test and evaluation (including whether the developmental test and evaluation and operational test are conducted in combination under the proviso of Article 21(3) of the Act and the replacement of the test evaluation under paragraph (5) of the same Article);
  5. Schedules for project implementation;
  6. Administration schemes covering the whole life cycle of weapons systems;
  7. In cases of developing required operational capability, step-by-step goals and strategies for the development thereof;
  8. Interoperability among the weapons systems of each service branch under the condition of joint battlefield.
  (3) The types of research and development referred to in paragraph (2)2 shall be classified as follows:
  1. Domestic research and development (including cases of receiving international technological cooperation) or joint international research and development;
  2. Research and development funded by the Government, research and development funded by defense contractors, etc., or joint research and development funded by the Government, defense contractors, etc.;
  3. Research and development supervised by the Agency for Defense Development or by defense contractors, etc. through competitive bidding.
  (4) Methods of purchasing referred to in paragraph (2)2 shall be classified into domestic purchase, offshore purchase, and lease. <Added on Oct. 1, 2010>
  [Title Amended on Jun. 30, 2026]

Article 24-2 (Procedures for pilot projects) (1) Procedures for implementing a pilot project under Article 17-2(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "pilot project") shall be in the following order:
  1. Public offering;
  2. Selection of eligible projects;
  3. Selection of project implementers;
  4. Development of prototypes;
  5. Model operations;
  6. Tests for evaluating the results of pilot projects pursuant to Article 17-2(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "performance test").
  (2) To conduct the performance test, the Chief of Staff of each service branch and the Commander of the Marine Corps shall formulate a performance test plan including the following:
  1. Equipment and quantity for a performance test;
  2. Test items of a performance test and criteria for evaluation;
  3. Organization of a performance test team;
  4. Budgets needed for a performance test.
  (3) The Chief of Staff of each service branch and the Commander of the Marine Corps shall notify the result of the performance test to the Minister of National Defense, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration and the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff.
  (4) A performance test team referred to in Article 17-2(2) of the Act shall have one team leader, who shall be appointed as a field-grade officer, and may have a performance team, a support team, etc.
  (5) The Agency under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense may request the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Chiefs of Staff of each service branch, or the Commander of the Marine Corps to implement a pilot project, if there is a requirement for a pilot project.
  (6) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (5), detailed procedures for a pilot project and the composition, operation, etc. of a performance test team shall be prescribed by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]
  [Previous Article 24-2 moved to Article 24-3 <Aug. 16, 2023>]

Article 24-3 (Methods of purchase) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration purchases military supplies produced in the Republic of Korea pursuant to the main clause of Article 19(1) of the Act, he or she may purchase a weapons system under development or developed in the Republic of Korea with partial remodeling.
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration purchases military supplies produced overseas pursuant to the proviso of Article 19(1) of the Act, he or she may purchase a weapons system under operation or under development in foreign countries with partial remodeling. <Amended on May 28, 2018>
  (3) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may lease military supplies where lease is deemed more economical than domestic purchase or offshore purchase or where it is necessary to satisfy the timing of fielding, etc.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 1, 2010]
  [Moved from Article 24-2; previous Article 24-3 moved to Article 24-4 <Aug. 16, 2023>]

Article 24-4 (International negotiation support officer and site supervisors of overseas projects) (1) If necessary for purchasing the military supplies manufactured overseas under the proviso of Article 19(1) of the Act, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may have public officials under his or her jurisdiction perform duties such as negotiation, support for conclusion of contracts and management of execution thereof overseas. <Added on May 28, 2018>
  (2) If necessary for performing any of the following duties, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may dispatch competent public officials overseas to supervise the implementation of the relevant contracts pursuant to Article 13 of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party: <Amended on May 28, 2018>
  1. Joint international research and development projects (including domestic research and development projects with international technical cooperation) referred to in Article 24(3)1;
  2. Purchase from a foreign country referred to in Article 24(4).
  (3) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may subsidize expenses, within budgetary limits, for the competent public officials who perform the duties prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2). <Amended on May 28, 2018>
  [This Article Added on Nov. 4, 2014]
  [Title Amended on May 28, 2018]
  [Moved from Article 24-3 <Aug. 16, 2023>]

Article 25 (Qualifications for participants in negotiation) (1) Civilian specialists permitted to participate in purchase procedures for military supplies pursuant to Article 19(2) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Aug. 9, 2022>
  1. A public official who has at least five years’ work experience in the division of international contracts or international negotiations;
  2. A person who has obtained the qualification of a lawyer, patent attorney, or certified public accountant and has at least three years of experience in the field of international contracts or international negotiations (including experience prior to obtaining the qualification);
  3. A person who has at least a master's degree in a field relating to an international contract or international negotiation, such as international trade and international commerce, and has at least five years’ work experience in international contracts or international negotiations (including experience prior to obtaining the qualification);
  4. A person who has at least 10 years’ work experience in the field of science and technology.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall select up to 15 civilian specialists prescribed in paragraph (1) upon recommendation by the Committee, and then have them participate in purchasing military supplies.

Article 25-2 (Procedures for purchasing munitions manufactured in Korea) (1) Military supplies manufactured in the Republic of Korea referred to in the main clause of Article 19(1) of the Act shall be purchased in the following order:
  1. Formulation of a purchase plan;
  2. Bid announcement;
  3. Selection of qualified contractors through testing, evaluation, eligibility examination, etc.;
  4. Conclusion of a purchase contract.
  (2) Detailed procedures for selecting qualified contractors under paragraph (1)3 shall be determined by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, taking into consideration the nature of each purchase contract.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 17, 2013]
  [Previous Article 25-2 moved to Article 25-3 <Dec. 17, 2013>]

Article 25-3 (Procedures for purchasing munitions manufactured in foreign countries) (1) Military supplies manufactured overseas referred to in the proviso of Article 19(1) of the Act shall be purchased in the following order: <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013>
  1. Formulation of a purchase plan;
  2. Bid announcement;
  3. Receipt and evaluation of proposals;
  4. Selection of weapons systems or equipment subject to testing and evaluation (referring to testing and evaluation specified in Article 21 of the Act; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article);
  5. Testing, evaluation, and negotiations;
  6. Determination on the model of the weapons systems or equipment subject to purchase;
  7. Conclusion of a purchase contract.
  (2) Weapons systems or equipment subject to testing and evaluation referred to in paragraph (1)4 shall be selected, based on the results of evaluation of proposals referred to in paragraph (1)3, but no priority shall be given according to the results of evaluation.
  (3) Negotiations referred to in paragraph (1)5 mean negotiations on the purchase price, the performance of weapons systems or equipment, offset trade, and other terms and conditions of contracts, and may proceed simultaneously with testing and evaluation. <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013>
  (4) The model of the weapons systems or equipment subject to purchase referred to in paragraph (1)6 shall be determined after comprehensively reviewing the results of testing, evaluation, and negotiations.
  (5) Evaluation methods for determination on models referred to in paragraph (4) and other matters necessary for procedures for purchase shall be determined by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 1, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 17, 2013]
  [Moved from Article 25-2 <Dec. 17, 2013>]

Article 26 (Standards for offset trade) (1) The amount of each unit project of military supplies for which offset trade shall be promoted pursuant to Article 20(1) of the Act shall be at least 10 million US dollars; provided, offset trade may be omitted in any of the following cases: <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; Mar. 30, 2021>
  1. Where parts for repair are purchased;
  1-2. Where core parts are purchased to be used for the defense research and development projects under Article 8 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  2. Where raw materials, such as petroleum, are purchased;
  2-2. Deleted; <Dec. 23, 2025>
  3. Where a purchase is made after deliberation by the Committee, in consideration of national security, economic efficiency, etc.
  (2) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as export linkages for commodities other than military supplies" in Article 20(3)6 of the Act means the following: <Added on Jul. 1, 2009; Mar. 23, 2013; Dec. 17, 2013; Jul. 26, 2017; Mar. 29, 2024; Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Export linkages for commodities, other than military supplies, selected by the Minister of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration upon recommendation of the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources, the Minister of SMEs and Startups, or the Minister of the Korea Aerospace Administration;
  2. Inducement of foreign investments (limited to foreign investments defined in Article 2(1)4 of the Foreign Investment Promotion Act) determined by the Minister of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, in consultation with the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources for the improvement of the competitiveness of the defense industry.
  (3) Matters necessary for the promotion of offset trade, such as procedures for promotion of offset trade, shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Amended on Jul. 1, 2009>

Article 27 (Formulation of test and evaluation plan) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense intends to formulate a test and evaluation plan for the research and development among tests and evaluations of weapons systems and core technologies pursuant to Article 21(1) and (3) of the Act, he or she shall formulate a test and evaluation master plan including the following matters; provided, where he or she deems it unnecessary to formulate a test and evaluation master plan, as prescribed by him or her, he or she need not formulate the test and evaluation master plan: <Amended on Jul. 19, 2016>
  1. Outline of weapons systems subject to test and evaluation;
  2. Outline of developmental test and evaluation (hereafter in this Article, referred to as "developmental test and evaluation") specified in Article 21(3)1 of the Act;
  3. Outline of operational test and evaluation (hereafter in this Article, referred to as "operational test and evaluation") specified in Article 21(3)2 of the Act;
  4. Resources necessary for test and evaluation;
  5. Other matters necessary for test and evaluation.
  (2) The Minister of National Defense shall formulate a developmental test and evaluation plan and an operational test and evaluation plan based on a test and evaluation master plan under paragraph (1); provided, where he or she deems it unnecessary to undergo both the developmental test and evaluation plan and the operational test and evaluation plan, he or she may choose not to formulate part of the plans. <Amended on Jul. 19, 2016>
  (3) Where the Minister of National Defense intends to formulate a test and evaluation plan for the purchase of weapons systems among tests and evaluations of weapons systems pursuant to Article 21(1) and (4) of the Act, he or she shall formulate a test and evaluation plan in accordance with the classification under the subparagraphs of paragraph (4) of that Article; provided, test and evaluation plan shall not be established if it is deemed unnecessary to establish a test evaluation plan as determined by the Minister of National Defense. <Added on Jul. 19, 2016; Aug. 16, 2023>
  (4) A test and evaluation plan referred to in paragraphs (2) and (3) shall contain the following matters: <Amended on Jul. 19, 2016>
  1. Items of equipment to be tested and evaluated, and quantities thereof;
  2. The timing, place, and method of a test and evaluation;
  3. Items of a test and evaluation, and the standards for evaluation;
  4. Testers and evaluators;
  5. Budgets needed for a test and evaluation.
  (5) Where the Minister of National Defense formulates a test and evaluation plan pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall consider the following matters, and where he or she formulates a test and evaluation plan pursuant to paragraph (3), he or she shall consider a written request for proposal: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010; Dec. 17, 2013; Nov. 4, 2014; Jul. 19, 2016>
  1. A plan for research and development;
  2. A proposal for a project for research and development by an enterprise;
  3. An execution plan for a test and evaluation for development prepared by a research and development institute;
  4. An execution plan for a test and evaluation for operations prepared by an agency proposing requirements.
  (6) For the purposes of the efficient formulation of plans for a test and evaluation, confirmation of the progress of a test and evaluation, judgment of results, etc., the Minister of National Defense may operate a combined test and evaluation team consisting of staff members of the Ministry of National Defense, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, each service branch, the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, and each agency referred to in Article 21(2) of the Act, and matters regarding the composition and operation thereof shall be prescribed by the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Jul. 19, 2016>
  (7) Where each service branch or a relevant agency tests or evaluates weapons systems and core technology under Article 21(2) of the Act, the Minister of National Defense shall secure and provide the budget necessary for smoothly conducting a test and evaluation. <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014; Jul. 19, 2016>
  (8) The Minister of National Defense may conduct a test and evaluation based on data pursuant to the latter part, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 21(4) of the Act in the following cases: <Added on Jul. 19, 2016; Aug. 16, 2023; Dec. 23, 2025>
  1. Where he or she purchases a weapons system which has no pilot product because it is being developed;
  2. Where he or she repurchases or purchases a partly remodeled weapons system currently in operation in the Republic of Korea;
  3. Where he or she purchases a weapons system currently in operation in order to integrate it into the complex weapons system, such as naval vessels and aircraft;
  4. Where weapons systems are purchased under a contract concluded with a foreign government.
  (9) Matters necessary for conducting a test and evaluation, such as procedures for test and evaluation, shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Jul. 19, 2016>
  [Title Amended on Jul. 19, 2016]

Article 27-2 (Formulation of plans for combined implementation of developmental test and evaluation and operational test and evaluation) Matters necessary for the formulation of a plan for combined implementation of the developmental test and evaluation and operational test and evaluation under the proviso of Article 21(3) of the Act and for the combined implementation shall be separately determined by the Minister of National Defense, notwithstanding the provisions of Article 27.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 16, 2023]

Article 28 (Securing of support elements of force integration) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration and the military and agencies under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense (hereinafter referred to as "required forces") related to requirements shall secure support elements for force integration according to the following classifications: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration: Subparagraph 13a (limited to the elements required at the weapons system acquisition phase);
  2. Requirement Group: Subparagraph 13a2) of Article 3 of the Act.
  (2) Where necessary for securing support elements of force integration pursuant to paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may request the requirement-raising armed forces to provide assistance. In such cases, the requirement-raising armed forces shall cooperate therewith, in the absence of good cause.
  (3) Where the requirement-raising armed forces intend to secure support elements of force integration, such as formulation of military units or education and training, etc. following the deployment of weapons systems, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may assist with the necessary budget, and where the requirement-raising armed forces agree, the Minister may have a defense contractor, etc. execute all or part of the follow-up assistance with materials for a certain period under contract.
  (4) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may present guidelines of the result, such as the target rate of operation for the main weapons systems, after consultation with the requirement-raising armed forces so that a defense contractor, etc. execute all or part of the follow-up assistance with materials, and may pay the price according to the result thereof. <Added on Jul. 1, 2009>

CHAPTER IV PROCUREMENT AND QUALITY ASSURANCE

Article 29 (Methods of procurement) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration procures military supplies in a lump pursuant to the main clause of Article 25(2) of the Act, he or she may procure military supplies by methods of domestic procurement, offshore procurement, or lease. In such cases, detailed procedures for procurement shall be determined by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (2) Military supplies that each service branch may directly procure pursuant to the proviso of Article 25(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "procurement by military unit") shall be as follows: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Pilot products developed directly by the military unit that manages military supplies;
  2. Consumable materials necessary for the maintenance of military supplies performed by a military maintenance unit (including the military maintenance depot; hereinafter the same shall apply), and for the manufacture of necessary parts therefor;
  3. Items the planned amount of annual procurement of which by item is less than 30 million won, and unitary items with a track record of procurement by the military unit among items the planned amount of annual procurement of which by item is between 30 million won and 50 million won;
  4. Items for which there are no national defense standards pursuant to Article 31(1) and no sample is available for presentation;
  5. Goods under subparagraph 15d of Article 3 of the Act;
  6. Items which require the maintenance of security, such as encryption equipment used by each service branch, and items which require special manufacture and installation because the requirements of users are diverse;
  7. Where equipment and parts procured by a military unit are serviced by entrusting an enterprise with such service, items necessary therefor;
  8. Other items agreed with the requirement-raising armed forces because the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deems the procurement by military unit is efficient.
  (3) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration purchases military supplies by requesting the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service pursuant to the proviso of Article 25(2) of the Act, he or she shall decide the matters concerning military supplies subject to purchase, and methods of and procedures for purchase in consultation with the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service. In such cases, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall hear an opinion of the requirement-raising armed forces.
  (4) When purchasing military supplies by requesting to the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service under the proviso of Article 25(2) of the Act, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall entrust the procurement of the following military supplies to the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service. <Added on Jul. 1, 2020>
  1. Commercial items from among combat support equipment provided for in subparagraph 1 of Article 2-2;
  2. Combat support materials referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 2-2;
  3. Medical support materials referred to in subparagraph 3 of Article 2-2.
  (5) Notwithstanding paragraph (4), any of the following military supplies that are necessary for operations, training and security may be excluded from those entrusted, in consultation with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration: <Added on Jul. 1, 2020>
  1. Military supplies procured in parallel with equipment on the grounds of a close relationship to the weapons system or for an efficient maintenance and management of equipment;
  2. Military supplies that require special attention for security, such as disguised networks, encryption equipment and military maps;
  3. Military supplies purchased from foreign countries by utilizing the foreign government' overseas military sales system or the like;
  4. Other military supplies for which direct procurement by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration is deemed efficient in terms of operations, training and security.
  (6) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall perform the standardization of military supplies under Article 26 of the Act procured by entrustment to the Administrator of the Public Procurement Service pursuant to paragraph (4) and the management of military supply catalog information under Article 27 of the Act. <Added on Jul. 1, 2020; Feb. 11, 2022>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Oct. 1, 2010]

Article 30 (Designation and cancellation of standard items) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration formulates a plan for the standardization of military supplies regarding force support systems pursuant to Article 26(1) of the Act, he or she shall seek opinions of the Minister of National Defense and reflect them in the plan. <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014>
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to designate standard military supply items, he or she shall consider the following matters. In such cases, the standard items for force support systems acquired by purchase shall be designated in consideration of the opinions of each service branch, and the standard items for force support systems acquired by research and development shall be designated upon request by the Ministry of National Defense or each service branch: <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014>
  1. Appropriateness of the purchase requirements by each service branch, and necessity of designation of standard articles;
  2. Economic efficiency of the relevant military supplies;
  3. Fulfillment of requirements for support elements of force integration;
  4. Degree of utilization in civilian and military fields;
  5. Interoperability with the military supplies in use.
  (3) In any of the following cases, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall cancel the designation of standard military supply items:
  1. Where military supplies are replaced by new standard items;
  2. Where it is unnecessary to maintain a standard item as it fails to satisfy the military utility and is economically inappropriate;
  3. Where it is necessary to replace the military supplies with the items, etc. manufactured and distributed among civilians.
  (4) Procedures necessary for the designation and cancellation of standard articles pursuant to paragraphs (2) and (3) shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.

Article 31 (Enactment and amendment of national defense standards) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to enact the standards of military supplies pursuant to Article 26(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "national defense standard"), he or she shall target the designated standard items, upon request for enactment by the following organizations: <Amended on Nov. 4, 2014>
  1. The Agency for Defense Development, in cases of research and development items under the supervision of the Agency for Defense Development;
  2. The relevant enterprise, in cases of research and development items under the supervision of the enterprise, or items, etc. procured en bloc by the Defense Acquisition Program Administration pursuant to Article 25 of the Act;
  3. The Ministry of National Defense or each service branch, in cases of items of force support systems procured by the Ministry of National Defense or each service branch.
  (2) For items whose national defense standard is not enacted, but procured by each service branch, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may have each service branch enact the standards thereof.
  (3) Where the effectiveness of the national defense standard has decreased due to changes in and development of the domestic industrial technology that manufactures military supplies, or changes in the national defense standard that each service branch requests, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may amend or abrogate the national defense standard.
  (4) Procedures necessary for the enactment, amendment, and abrogation of national defense standards pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (3) shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.

Article 32 (Configuration management) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall, in order to ensure that military supplies maintain their intended utility, manage military supplies based on their physical or functional characteristics under Article 26(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "configuration") as follows:
  1. Identification of the configuration to ensure that military supplies meet the performance requirements of each service branch and maximize operational efficiency, etc.;
  2. Documentation of the configuration, including the preparation of design drawings, to give concrete form to the identified configuration;
  3. Verification, coordination, and control of configuration details to be modified on the basis of the documented configuration;
  4. Verification of whether military supplies conform to the identified configuration and management of relevant data.
  (2) The configuration management of military supplies shall be executed from the time when the method of project promotion regarding the method of acquisition of weapons systems, etc. pursuant to the provisions of Article 17 of the Act is decided until the military supplies become obsolete pursuant to the provisions of Article 13(3) of the Act on the Management of Military Supplies.
  (3) The detailed procedures necessary for configuration management shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.

Article 33 (Munitions inventory information) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to allocate a stock number to military supplies pursuant to the provisions of Article 27(1) of the Act, he or she shall do so by classifying them in accordance with the drawing, price, packing unit, period of storage, and information, etc. on the manufacturer.
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration formulates a plan for the administration and utilization of military supply catalog information pursuant to the provisions of Article 27(2) of the Act, he or she shall hear and reflect the opinion of the Minister of the National Defense.
  (3) Where a relevant organization, such as each service branch, an agency under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense, the Agency for Defense Development, and the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, needs to correct or supplement military supply catalog information, it shall submit the relevant details to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall decide whether to correct or supplement the information and notify the result to the relevant organization.
  (4) Where defense materials are exported, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may conclude an agreement on the exchange of military supply catalog information that can be supplied to the export partner country.

CHAPTER V ADVANCEMENT OF DEFENSE SCIENCE AND TECHNOLOGY

