Article 50 (Protection and fostering of defense contractors) (1) The Government shall preferentially purchase defense materials manufactured by defense contractors.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall notify the relevant defense contractors annually of the quantity of defense materials planned for manufacturing.
  (3) Where the defense contractor intends to manufacture the annual quantity before the procurement contract of the pertinent year is concluded from among the planned manufacturing quantity notified pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (2), he or she shall obtain approval therefor from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (4) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to conclude a long-term contract, he or she shall notify the defense contractor concerned of quantities of defense materials planned for manufacturing by year and such defense contractor may secure raw materials and parts within budget of the relevant year by obtaining approval from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration prior to the conclusion of a procurement contract. <Added on Jan. 7, 2009; Apr. 30, 2024>
  (5) The defense contractor may request the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration to grant a quality certification of the materials manufactured pursuant to paragraph (3) and raw materials and parts secured pursuant to paragraph (4), and the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall comply therewith, in the absence of good cause. <Amended on Jan. 7, 2009>
  (6) Where there are materials manufactured pursuant to the provisions of paragraph (3) which are difficult to keep or are prone to safety accidents, etc., the defense contractor may deliver or keep the materials in the place determined by the relevant Chief of Staff after consultation with the armed force where such materials are to be delivered. <Amended on Jan. 7, 2009>

Article 51 Deleted. <Feb. 2, 2021>

Article 52 Deleted. <Feb. 2, 2021>

Article 53 Deleted. <Feb. 2, 2021>

Article 54 Deleted. <Mar. 30, 2021>

Article 55 (Assistance to defense industry) (1) When seeking technical support or production support under Article 41(1) of the Act, a defense contractor or specialized research institute shall submit an application for such support stating the following to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Chief of Staff of each service branch, the President of the Agency for Defense Development, the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality, or the head of a military maintenance unit:
  1. Details and period of the support requested;
  2. Details and conditions of cost burden.
  (2) Where a defense contractor intends to produce, modify, or develop defense materials and possess them under Article 41(3) of the Act, such contractor shall submit to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration an application for approval to possess defense materials in the form prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, stating the following:
  1. Total quantity of defense materials to be possessed and the purpose of possession for each quantity;
  2. Plan for the possession and management of defense materials;
  3. Schedule and process plan for the production, modification, or development of defense materials;
  4. Measures necessary to protect defense technology under Article 8(1) of the Defense Technology Security Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as "defense technology protection measures").
  (3) Upon receipt of an application for approval for possession under paragraph (2), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall review the following, determine whether to grant approval, and notify the relevant defense contractor of the result within 30 days from the date of receipt of the application:
  1. Appropriateness of the quantity of defense materials stated in the application;
  2. Appropriateness of the schedule and process plan for the production, modification, or development of defense materials;
  3. Appropriateness of defense technology protection measures;
  4. Impact on the fielding of weapons systems.
  (4) Where a defense contractor intends to dispose of defense materials possessed with the approval of the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration under Article 41(3) of the Act, such contractor shall submit to the Administrator an application for approval to dispose of defense materials in the form prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, stating the following:
  1. Specific reasons for the intended disposal;
  2. Method of disposal and detailed procedures.
  (5) Upon receipt of an application for approval for disposal under paragraph (4), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall review the appropriateness of the reasons for and method of disposal, determine whether to grant approval, and notify the relevant defense contractor of the result within 30 days from the date of receipt of the application.
  (6) If necessary to inspect whether a defense contractor possesses defense materials in accordance with the approval granted under paragraph (3) or has disposed of them in accordance with the approval granted under paragraph (5), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may verify the status of management of defense materials.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 23, 2025]

Article 56 Deleted. <Feb. 2, 2021>

Article 57 (Designation of guarantee organization) (1) Anyone who intends to be designated as a guarantee organization pursuant to Article 43(1) of the Act shall satisfy the requirements in the following subparagraphs:
  1. Paid-in capital (an endowment in the case of a nonprofit corporation) shall be 500 million won or more;
  2. To have sufficient human resources and material facilities for conducting guarantee businesses in the each of subparagraphs of Article 43(2) of the Act;
  3. To secure funds (hereinafter referred to as "guarantee funds") necessary for conducting guarantee business for defense contractors, etc.
  (2) Anyone who intends to be designated pursuant to paragraph (1) shall apply to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration after preparing the documents listed in the following subparagraphs. In such cases, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall verify a corporation registration certificate through administrative data matching referred to in Article 36(1) of the Electronic Government Act: <Amended on Jun. 28, 2007; May 4, 2010; Nov 2, 2010>
  1. An application for designation;
  2. Deleted; <Jun. 28, 2007>
  3. Documents that can verify the qualifications in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1);
  4. Documents regarding guarantee regulations, such as the scope of guarantees, the terms of guarantee contracts, the limits on guarantees, and guarantee fees.
  (3) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration has designated a guarantee organization pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall publicly announce it.

Article 58 Deleted. <Feb. 2, 2021>

Article 59 (Transfer and lease of State-owned property) (1) General assets that can be leased without consideration pursuant to Article 45(1) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Jul. 27, 2009; Mar. 30, 2021>
  1. Knowledge and information on defense science and technology under Article 12(1) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  2. Land, buildings, and structures provided directly for the transfer of defense facilities executed in accordance with an order pursuant to Article 49(1) of the Act.
  (2) Administrative assets that can be permitted for use without consideration pursuant to Article 45(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. All kinds of test centers and laboratory facilities;
  2. Explosives disposal sites;
  3. Shooting ranges;
  4. Other assets prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense as necessary for the manufacture and testing of defense materials.
  (3) Equipment for exclusive use or goods that can be leased free of charge or at a cost pursuant to Article 45(2) of the Act shall be raw materials, equipment, jigs and fixtures, measuring instruments, inspection machinery and tools, or articles and parts thereof for performance test and inspection, which are used for the manufacture and research of defense materials or manufacture of prototypes.
  (4) Cases where specialized equipment or goods can be leased with or without consideration under paragraph (3) shall be as follows: <Amended on May 11, 2021>
  1. Where the repair of disorder takes a long time or is impossible, or where it is apprehended to incur setbacks to the manufacture of defense materials due to difficulties in domestic purchase or in import from overseas;
  2. Where it is needed for the performance test or inspection of defense materials;
  3. Where it is impossible to manufacture defense materials as the manufacturing facilities have been destroyed due to natural disaster or other accidents;
  4. Where the lease thereof is stipulated as a condition in the procurement contract or in commissioning of research and development, etc. of defense materials;
  5. Where it is necessary for the test and evaluation of the results of projects for domestic development of components.
  (5) Equipment for exclusive use or goods that can be transferred free of charge under Article 45(2) of the Act are as follows: <Amended on May 11, 2021>
  1. Specialized equipment or goods that have been leased pursuant to Article 45(2) of the Act, but that cannot be returned or the return thereof is inappropriate after use as they fall under paragraph (4)2 or 3;
  2. Military supplies that are equipment determined by the military as useless or surplus articles, but that can be used after repair or recycled by defense contractors or specialized research institutes;
  3. Goods leased under paragraph (4)5, for which transferring is economically advantageous, considering the costs of restoration or storage for the return after the test and evaluation.
  (6) In any of the following cases, the Government shall preferentially consider transferring or leasing a facility owned by the State for manufacturing defense materials or lending defense materials owned by the State, without consideration, granting permission for the use of such facility or defense materials, or exchanging such defense materials with defense materials owned by a defense contractor pursuant to Article 45(4) of the Act: <Added on Dec. 17, 2013; May 11, 2021>
  1. Where a facility for manufacturing defense materials or defense materials become unusable because of aging or any other reason, and it is economically better to transfer such facility or defense materials without consideration, compared with the cost of shutdown or disposal;
  2. Where the appropriate circulation cycle for the maintenance of quality or performance of defense materials is anticipated to elapse, and it is necessary to replace such defense materials with new ones;
  3. Where it is anticipated to keep excessive quantity of certain defense materials in stock as a result of a decrease in the demand for the defense materials following a change in weapons systems, and it is necessary to replace such defense materials with other materials.
  (7) Where a defense contractor or a specialized research institute intends to use, borrow, or acquire State assets pursuant to paragraphs (1) through (6), it shall apply therefor to the relevant management agency along with the following documents after receiving recommendation by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration: <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; May 11, 2021>
  1. An application form;
  2. A plan for utilization.
  (8) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (7), the management agency shall permit the use thereof, lease or transfer, in the absence of good cause. <Amended on Dec. 17, 2013; May 11, 2021>
  [Title Amended on May 11, 2021]

Article 60 (Long-term contracts) (1) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall allow a person who intends to bid for a long-term contract to bid for all manufacturing or total services.
  (2) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration concludes a long-term contract, he or she shall conclude the contract by additionally stating the following amounts:
  1. The amount of a contract (referring to the total production amount determined by a successful tender, etc. or the total service amount, excluding the amount of materials supplied by the government office; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article);
  2. The Amount of annual distribution of the contract price under subparagraph 1 on an annual basis (hereafter referred to as "annual distribution amount" in this Article).
  (3) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall, after concluding a contract pursuant to paragraph (2), determine the annual distribution amount each year in consideration of the budget for the relevant year within the scope of maintaining the identity of the contract during the period of execution of the contract, and shall accumulate the determined annual distribution amount and additionally enter the determined annual distribution amount in the contract.
  (4) Article 37(1) and 50(3) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall apply mutatis mutandis to tender bonds, contract bonds, etc. for long-term contracts.
  (5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for the conclusion, etc. of long-term contracts shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61 (Types, terms, and methods of contracts) (1) Contracts referred to in Article 46(1) of the Act shall be concluded in accordance with the following classification: <Amended on Jul. 1, 2009; Oct. 1, 2010; Dec. 17, 2013>
  1. General conclusive contracts: Where the contract amount is agreed at the time of conclusion of the contract, and the agreed contract amount is to be paid to the party to the contract if the agreed contract terms are fulfilled;
  2. Commodity price-coordinated unit price contract: Where it is intended to conclude a contract for an item, for which a contract has been concluded during the latest three years after determining an estimated price by the cost calculation method, at a price of not more than the maximum contract amount determined by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration by adjusting the latest contract unit price by fluctuation rate of the index specified by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, such as the price indexes collected and published by the Bank of Korea under Article 86 of the Bank of Korea Act, without re-calculating the cost;
  3. Cost reduction compensation contract: Where the amount of cost reduction is deducted from the contract amount and the compensation within the extent of the cost reduction is intended, when there is new technology or development of engineering methods, business rationalization, etc. during the contract execution period after the conclusion of a contract;
  4. Cost reduction incentive contract: Where cost reduction can be expected in the nature of a contract or it is necessary to encourage cost reduction for substituting imported products with domestically-manufactured products, the contract is concluded with a targeted cost or profit specified for the items or components of the cost, the level of which the opposite party to the contract can control, and an incentive shall be paid in addition to the contract amount according to the results of the actual cost, the achievement of the targeted profit, and the reduction of the targeted cost after performing the contract;
  5. Ceiling amount contract: Where a ceiling amount is established in order to efficiently secure repair parts for main equipment and maintenance for the operation of weapons systems when a contract is concluded, and the repair parts and maintenance are to be requested from the contracting enterprise for a certain period within the scope of such ceiling amount;
  6. Midway conclusive contract: Where the contract amount is to be determined in the course of execution of a contract as it is difficult to determine the contract amount at the time of conclusion of a contract in view of the nature of a contract;
  7. Deleted; <Dec. 17, 2013>
  8. Indeterminate item contract: Where it is difficult to determine the cost of certain items that constitute the contract amount when concluding a contract, it is intended to determine only the items which are possible to determine the cost, and part of the items that are difficult to determine are to be determined after the execution of a contract;
  9. General approximation contract: Where the contract amount is to be determined after the execution of a contract as there is no cost data to determine the contract amount at the time of conclusion of a contract;
  10. Performance-based contract: Where it is required to accomplish a certain performance objective at the time of conclusion of a contract and the consideration is to be paid commensurate with performance after the contract execution;
  11. Long-term option contract: Where the period of a contract is specified, which shall not exceed five years, and the conditions of amendment with respect to the price for the estimated quantity of requirements, the period of a contract, the termination of a contract, etc. are stipulated, but a separate contract shall be concluded on the quantity that shall be purchased by enforcing the conditions of amendment;
  12. Ceiling performance contract: Where a ceiling for the supply of parts is fixed for a specified period of a contract, which shall not exceed five years, at the time a contract for the supply of parts for the repair of a weapons systems is concluded, and then the supplier is required to supply parts for repair as necessary within the ceiling, and the price therefor may vary according to the performance of supply.
  (2) Upon completion of the performance of a contract concluded under any of paragraph (1)3 through 6, 8 through 10, and 12, the contractor shall obtain approval therefor from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration under Article 70(3) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party; provided, upon completion of the performance of a contract concluded for procurement by a military unit, the contractor shall obtain approval therefor from the competent Chief of Staff or the competent head of an agency under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense, notwithstanding Article 70(3) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party. <Amended on Jul. 1, 2009; Dec. 17, 2013>
  (3) Article 7 of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall apply to the method of a defense acquisition contract under the latter part of Article 46(1) of the Act, and it may be concluded as a free contract in the following cases: <Added on Jul. 1, 2009; Dec. 17, 2013; Nov. 4, 2014; Apr. 14, 2015; Jan. 22, 2019; Feb. 2, 2021; Mar. 30, 2021; Feb. 11, 2022; Aug. 16, 2023; Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. Where a contract for the manufacture (referring to the manufacture under Article 2(1)2 of the Defense Industry Development and Support Act) or purchase of defense materials (including a contract for the manufacture or purchase of repair parts (limited to those necessary at the weapons system acquisition phase) under subparagraph 13b of Article 3 of the Act, which are not manufactured by the relevant defense contractor) is concluded with a defense contractor;
  1-2. Where a purchase contract is concluded with a person who implemented the pilot project, after being selected as a project implementer under Article 24-2(1), with regard to a weapons system the requirements for which are determined through the pilot project;
  2. Where a ceiling amount contract referred to in paragraph (1)5 is concluded;
  3. Where a performance-based contract referred to in paragraph (1)10 is concluded;
  4. Where a contract is concluded for a quantity purchased by enforcing the terms of amendment under a long-term option contract under paragraph (1)11;
  5. Where a contract for defense acquisition programs is concluded in connection with the goods referred to in subparagraph 15d of Article 3 of the Act;
  6. Where a contract is concluded for the present contract partner to continue the research or production of prototype pursuant to Article 8(1) and (4) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act before the mass production phase in order to meet the effective research and development of weapons systems or the time of reinforcing military capability;
  7. Where each of the following requirements is satisfied, where a contract is concluded for the research and development of the relevant core technology with a specialized research institute having submitted the subject of a research referred to in item b:
  a. Where the specialized research institute has submitted a research task in accordance with procedures for research and development of core technology under Article 8(7) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act with respect to the entrusted areas;
  b. Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration has determined to advance the research and development of core technology with respect to the research task referred to in item a;
  8. Where a contract is concluded with a domestic company that has developed the maintenance capability of equipment, components, or combinations, which is deemed necessary to develop domestic maintenance capability because there is no domestic repair clerk (repair clerk), as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense.
  (4) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraphs (1) through (3), matters necessary for the specific details, scope, conclusion, etc. of defense acquisition program contracts shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  (5) Deleted. <Apr. 30, 2024>
  (6) Deleted. <Apr. 30, 2024>

Article 61-2 (Determination of successful tenderer centered on performance) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deems it necessary for competitive bidding for the conclusion of a contract for a defense acquisition program pursuant to Article 46(3) of the Act, notwithstanding Article 42 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party, he or she may determine a successful bidder by comprehensively considering the performance or quality of the relevant goods or services in addition to the bid price or by preferentially considering the performance or quality of the relevant goods or services.
  (2) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to determine a successful tenderer under paragraph (1), he or she shall determine the criteria for evaluating the performance or quality of the relevant goods or services before tendering, and allow participants in tendering to inspect such criteria.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters necessary for the criteria, methods, procedures, etc. for determining successful bidders shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]
  [Previous Article 61-2 moved to Article 61-6 <Apr. 30, 2024>]

Article 61-3 (Standards and methods for determining budget prices) (1) Where it is necessary to determine a budget price in entering into a defense acquisition program contract (excluding contracts for overseas purchase; hereafter the same shall apply in Article 61-4, 61-5, and 61-7 through 61-9), Article 8 and 9 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall apply where it is necessary to determine the budget price.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the price by cost accounting under Article 9(1)2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall comply with the standards and methods for cost accounting prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense in consideration of the specificity of defense acquisition contracts.
  (3) Detailed Standards and Methods for Determining Budget Price under paragraph (1) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-4 (Settlement of accounts for contracts by rough estimate) (1) Where a contract for a defense acquisition program is concluded under an estimated contract pursuant to Article 46(5) of the Act, Article 70 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall apply to the determination of the estimated price, procedures for concluding the estimated contract, standards for settlement, etc.
  (2) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration determines the estimated price in advance pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall determine the estimated price based on the following prices; provided, where a contract is concluded through negotiations under Article 43 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party, the determination of the estimated price may be omitted:
  1. Quoted price;
  2. Price calculated by cost accounting of similar goods or services.
  (3) Notwithstanding the standards for settlement of accounts under paragraph (1), in cases of an estimated contract that meets all of the following requirements, an additional settlement may be made within the scope of 3/100 of the initial contract amount; provided, the additional settlement may not exceed the difference between the budget for the relevant project and the initial contract amount at the time of public notice of a tender:
  1. Where contracts are concluded through competitive tendering;
  2. Where the cost calculated at the time of settlement for reasons prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense on the grounds prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense for which the other party to the defense acquisition program is unable to control, exceeds the contract price at the time of settlement (referring to the contract price reflecting the relevant change in the contract price, if the contract price is changed after the contract is concluded).
  (4) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraphs (1) through (3), matters necessary for the standards for concluding estimated contracts, detailed standards for settling accounts of estimated contracts, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of the National Defense. <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-5 (Preferential acquisition of domestically obtained raw materials) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration enters into a contract for a defense acquisition program pursuant to Article 46(6) of the Act, he or she may reflect the following matters in the items of assessment of proposals for determining a successful bidder so that raw materials, materials, parts, products, etc. (hereafter referred to as "raw materials, etc." in this Article) manufactured in the Republic of Korea may be preferentially acquired:
  1. Matters related to preferential acquisition of domestically produced raw materials or similar materials;
  2. Where information is likely to be divulged or infringed due to the use of foreign-made raw materials, etc., measures to prevent the use of such raw materials, etc. and measures to secure, handle, and manage such raw materials, etc. where foreign-made raw materials, etc. are used.
  (2) Matters necessary for the methods, procedures, etc. for evaluating proposals under paragraph (1) shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-6 (Selective contracts for military supplies) (1) A person, who intends to be the partner of a contract under Article 46(7) the Act (hereinafter referred to as "option contract for military supplies"), shall be qualified as a competitive bidder under Article 12 of Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party; provided, a person who intends to be the partner of an option contract for military supplies with respect to competitive products among small and medium companies referred to in Article 6 of the Act on Facilitation of Purchase of Small and Medium Enterprise-Manufactured Products and Support for Development of Their Markets shall be qualified as a competitive bidder among small and medium companies under Article 9 of Enforcement Decree of that Act. <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  (2) The partner of an option contract for military supplies shall be a successful bidder determined by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration by evaluating the performance of delivery, management condition, credit rating, etc. among persons qualified under paragraph (1).
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters necessary for procedures for option contracts for military supplies shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Nov. 4, 2014]
  [Moved from Article 61-2 <Apr. 30, 2024>]

Article 61-7 (Standards and methods for payment of subscription amount and intermediate payments) (1) In any of the following cases, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may pay a retainer and intermediate payment under Article 46-2(1) of the Act:
  1. Where the remaining implementation period of a defense acquisition program contract (excluding a purchase contract; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) exceeds 30 days from the date on which the payment of the retainer and intermediate payment is requested;
  2. Where it is deemed impracticable to perform a contract unless the retainer or intermediate payment is paid even though it does not fall under the remaining period of performance of the defense acquisition program contract under subparagraph 1.
  (2) The report and intermediate payments shall be paid within the limits appropriated in the budget for the relevant year, but the total amount of payment by aggregating the report and intermediate payments shall not exceed 90/100 of the contract amount; provided, where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deems it necessary to overcome the economic crisis caused by a disaster, economic recession, mass unemployment, etc. under subparagraph 1 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety, the report and intermediate payments may be paid in excess of 90/100 after consultation with the Minister of Finance and Economy. <Amended on Dec. 30, 2025>
  (3) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall calculate the advance payment and intermediate payment based on the amount obtained by deducting the price from the contract amount in cases of a defense acquisition program contract in which consideration is paid for the completed or already paid portion pursuant to Article 15 of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party.
  (4) In any of the following cases after the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration has paid a retainer payment and an intermediate payment, he or she shall, without delay, request the other party to the defense acquisition program contract to return all or part of the retainer payment and the intermediate payment:
  1. Where the relevant defense acquisition program contract is cancelled or terminated;
  2. Where he or she violates any of the conditions prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense concerning the payment, etc. of advance payments and intermediate payments.
  (5) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall not pay any advance payment or intermediate payment where the other party to a contract for defense acquisition programs is subject to restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering pursuant to Article 59 of the Act, Article 70 of this Decree, and Article 27 of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party and is in the period of such restrictions.
  (6) Where the other party to a defense acquisition program contract falls under any of the following cases pursuant to the main clause of Article 46-2(3) of the Act, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may pay a retainer and an intermediate payment to the other defense acquisition program contract concluded before he or she is subject to restriction on qualification for participation in a tendering procedure:
  1. Where he or she is subject to restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering, irrespective of a national defense procurement contract;
  2. Where he or she is subject to restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering for less than two years in connection with a national defense procurement contract.
  (7) Where the other party to a defense acquisition program contract falls under any subparagraph of paragraph (6), the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may pay a differential advance payment and intermediate payment according to the following classifications pursuant to the proviso of Article 46-2(3) of the Act:
  1. Where the other party to a defense acquisition program contract falls under paragraph (6)1: Full payment of the amount calculated by subtracting the already paid retainer and intermediate payment from the maximum amount of the retainer and intermediate payment that can be paid pursuant to paragraphs (2) and (3);
  2. Where the other party to a defense acquisition program contract falls under paragraph (6)2: Payment differentially by at least 30/100 of the amount calculated by subtracting the already paid retainers and intermediate payments from the maximum amount of the retainers and intermediate payments that can be paid pursuant to paragraphs (2) and (3).
  (8) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraphs (1) through (7), matters necessary for the detailed standards for payment and return of advance payments and intermediate payments, differential standards for payment, etc. shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-8 (Provision of incentives for application of core technology) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration applies core technologies, future-leading defense technologies, new technologies, etc. defined in subparagraph 5b through d of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as "core technologies, etc.") to the subject matter of a contract pursuant to Article 46-3(1) of the Act, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may grant incentives, such as additional points, in consideration of the type of core technologies, etc., whether they are applied, the degree of utilization, etc., when evaluating a proposal for determining a successful tenderer.
  (2) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to grant incentives pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall specify whether to grant additional points to the application of core technology, etc. when publicly announcing a tender, and the standards therefor, etc.
  (3) Matters necessary for the methods, procedures, etc. for evaluating proposals under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-9 (Imposition of compensation for deferment) (1) The compensation for deferment under Article 46-4(1) of the Act shall be the amount calculated by multiplying the contract amount by the number of days of delay and the rate prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  (2) In cases falling under paragraph (1), where a person takes over (including where he or she manages and uses before taking over before taking over; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) the completed portion or the portion paid in advance after undergoing an inspection, the penalty for delay shall be calculated based on the amount calculated by deducting the amount equivalent to such portion from the contract amount. In such cases, the takeover of the completed portion or the portion paid in advance shall be limited to the completed portion of goods, services, etc. that may be divided in light of their nature.
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraphs (1) and (2), in cases of any of the following contracts, the limit on the total amount of liquidated damages for delay shall be as prescribed in paragraph (4): <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024; Dec. 23, 2025>
  1. A contract for conducting a research and development project for a weapons system under subparagraph 5a of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act (in cases of a mass-production contract, limited to a contract for the mass production of defense materials);
  2. The agreement conducting research and development of core technology under subparagraph 5b of Article 2 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act.
  (4) The limit of the total amount of compensation for delay under paragraph (3) shall be as follows:
  1. Where the contract period expires on or after May 1, 2024 and the compensation for deferment accrues as a result of the expiration of the contract period: An amount equivalent to 10/100 of the contract amount (referring to the amount deducted from the contract amount where the portion completed or the portion already paid is acquired through an inspection pursuant to paragraph (2); hereafter the same shall apply in this paragraph);
  2. Where liquidated damages for delay are incurred due to the expiration of the contract period before May 1, 2024, the amount classified as follows:
  a. A contract falling under any subparagraph of Article 61(4) of the previous Enforcement Decree of the Defense Acquisition Program Act (referring to the Enforcement Decree of the Defense Acquisition Program Act (referring to the Enforcement Decree of the Defense Acquisition Program Act prior to being amended by Presidential Decree No. 34459): An amount equivalent to 10/100 of the contract price;
  b. Contracts other than those referred to in item a: An amount equivalent to 30/100 of the contract price.
  (5) In applying Article 75(2)2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party to a contract falling under any subparagraph of paragraph (3), the limit of a contract bond shall be the amount obtained by adding the maximum amount of compensation for deferment under paragraph (4) to the original contract bond.
  (6) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (5), matters necessary for detailed procedures, etc. for imposing liquidated damages for delay shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-10 (Reduction of or exemption from penalty for delay) (1) "Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 46-4(2)1 of the Act means any of the following cases:
  1. Natural disasters, incidents, emergencies, emergency disasters, infectious diseases, or other similar reasons;
  2. Government is responsible for such reasons as changes in policies of the Government, failures in computerization, administrative delays, etc.;
  3. Delay in importation of major raw materials or components due to war of exporting state of major raw materials or components, border closure, prohibition of exportation, etc.
  (2) Pursuant to Article 46-4(2) of the Act, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may reduce or exempt compensation for delay within the following limits:
  1. In cases falling under Article 46-4(2)1 of the Act: Full reduction of or exemption from the liquidated damages for delay on the relevant delayed date;
  2. In cases falling under Article 46-4(2)2 of the Act and subparagraph 3c of the same paragraph: Reduction of at least 30/100 of the amount of liquidated damages for the relevant delayed date;
  3. In cases falling under Article 46-4(2)3a and b of the Act: Reduction or exemption as determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, taking into consideration whether an appropriate subcontractor, etc. exists, other than a subcontractor who has a cause of delay, as determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (3) The other party to a defense acquisition program contract who intends to be fully or partially reduced or exempted from compensation for delay pursuant to Article 46-4(2) of the Act shall file an application for reduction or exemption with the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, along with materials, etc. evidencing the grounds for delay in performing the contract.
  (4) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration upon receipt of an application for reduction or exemption of compensation for delay under paragraph (3), he or she shall determine whether to grant reduction or exemption of compensation for delay, taking into consideration the nature and scale of the subject matter of the contract, the level of fidelity to perform the contract, etc. and notify the other party to the defense acquisition program contract of the compensation for delay calculated accordingly. If the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deems it necessary in consideration of the grounds for reduction or exemption of the compensation for delay, he or she may undergo deliberation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee.
  (5) Notwithstanding paragraph (4), where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration applies for the reduction or exemption of compensation for delay on the grounds falling under Article 46-4(2)2 and 3c of the Act, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall determine whether to grant the reduction or exemption of compensation for delay, following deliberation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee after hearing the opinion of the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality.
  (6) Where the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality intends to submit his or her opinion pursuant to paragraph (5), he or she may organize and operate a technology review committee comprised of the Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, each service branch, the Agency for Defense Development, civilian experts in the field of defense acquisition programs, etc. in order to professionally review matters concerning the challenges, sincerity, etc. of the performance of contracts and gather consensus from interested parties, etc.
  (7) The Technology Review Committee referred to in paragraph (6) may request the Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, each service branch, etc. to submit data, if necessary for efficiently performing its duties. Upon receipt of a request for submission of data in such cases, a person in receipt of such request shall comply therewith, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (8) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraphs (1) through (7), matters necessary for the detailed standards and procedures for the reduction of or exemption from liquidated damages for delay and the composition, operation, etc. of the technology review committee shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-11 (Change of contract) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to modify the period, amount, conditions, etc. of a contract (including an estimated contract; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) pursuant to the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 46-5 of the Act, he or she may undergo deliberation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee, if deemed necessary in consideration of the scale of the contract, the clarity of the grounds for modification, etc.
  (2) Where the period, amount, conditions, etc. of a contract may be modified pursuant to the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 46-5 of the Act and subparagraph 1 of the same Article, the following cases shall be deemed included:
  1. Change of process schedule;
  2. Change in characteristics of materials;
  3. Technology change;
  4. Reflection of additional requirements by requirement-proposing agency;
  5. Where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deems that the cases fall under subparagraph 1 of Article 46-5 of the Act, which are equivalent to those referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 4.
  (3) Where the other party to a defense acquisition program contract intends to request the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration to modify the contract on the grounds referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 46-5 of the Act, the other party may request the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration to modify the contract, as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, after undergoing testing, evaluation, etc. under Article 21 of the Act in relation to the terms and conditions of the contract to be changed.
  (4) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), where the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to modify a contract for any reason prescribed in subparagraph 2 of Article 46-5 of the Act, he or she shall hear the opinion of the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality and submit it for deliberation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee. Article 61-10(6) and (7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the submission, etc. of opinion of the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality in such cases.
  (5) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraphs (1) through (4), the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall prescribe matters necessary for the specific details, procedures, etc. for the modification of a contract and other necessary matters; provided, matters concerning the adjustment of the contract price in the estimated contract shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 61-12 (Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee) (1) The Ministry of National Defense or the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may establish Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee in order to efficiently deliberate on and seek advice on the following matters concerning bidding or successful tenders for defense acquisition program contracts or the conclusion and execution of contracts:
  1. Whether a tendering procedure, successful tendering procedure, or contract under the proviso of Article 6(4) of the Act and Article 4(10) of this Decree continues to be executed;
  2. Matters concerning reduction of or exemption from liquidated damages for delay under Article 46-4(2) of the Act and Article 61-10(4) and (5) of this Decree;
  3. Matters concerning change of contracts under Article 46-5 of the Act and Article 61-11(1) and (4) of this Decree;
  4. Matters concerning restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering procedures under Article 59(1) of the Act and Article 70(1) of this Decree;
  5. Other matters the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deems necessary for deliberation or consultation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee.
  (2) The Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee shall be comprised of not more than 15 members, including one chairperson.
  (3) The chairperson of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee shall be appointed by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration from among the members of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee, and the members of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee shall be appointed or commissioned by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration from among the following persons:
  1. Persons appointed by the heads of the agencies to which they belong, from among public officials who are at least equivalent to director general level or field-grade officers of the Ministry of National Defense, the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the Joint Chiefs of Staff, and each service branch;
  2. Persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in defense acquisition programs, procurement contracts, etc.
  (4) The term of office of members of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee shall be two years, and they may be reappointed only once; provided, the term of office of a member under paragraph (3)1 shall be the period during which he or she holds the relevant position.
  (5) A majority of the members of the Defense Acquisition Program Contract Deliberation Committee shall constitute a quorum, and any decision thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present; provided, in cases of deliberation on paragraph (1)2, a majority of the members of the Defense Acquisition Program Contract Deliberation Committee shall constitute a quorum, and any decision thereof shall require the concurring vote of at least 2/3 of those present.
  (6) Article 14-2, 14-3, 17, and 18 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the exclusion, challenge, abstention, refrainment, dismissal, request for submission of materials, allowances, travel expenses, etc. of members of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee.
  (7) The Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee may establish a subcommittee if necessary to efficiently deliberate on and consult on matters delegated by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee among the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1). <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024>
  (8) The chairperson and members of a subcommittee shall be appointed by the chairperson of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration Contract Deliberation Committee from among the members of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee. <Added on Jul. 16, 2024>
  (9) Matters referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) that have undergone deliberation by a subcommittee in accordance with the delegation of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee shall be deemed to have undergone deliberation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee. <Added on Jul. 16, 2024>
  (10) Paragraph (5) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation of meetings of the subcommittee. <Added on Jul. 16, 2024>
  (11) The Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee shall continue to exist until Apr. 30, 2029. <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024>
  (12) Except as otherwise expressly provided for in paragraphs (1) through (11), matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee and subcommittees shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 62 (Procedures for revocation of designation of defense contractors) Where the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources revokes designation of a defense contractor pursuant to Article 48(1) of the Act, he or she shall notify the Minister of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration and the relevant defense contractor thereof by specifying the grounds therefor, and retrieve the certificate of designation of the defense contractor. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 63 (Termination of commission of specialized research institutes) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may terminate the commission where a specialized research institute falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
  1. Where it fails to meet the requirements for security pursuant to Article 44;
  2. Where it refuses or fails to execute the commission for research and development of defense materials or manufacturing of prototypes, etc. without justifiable grounds;
  3. Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration deems that there is no need to retain it as a specialized research institute due to the insufficiency of research facilities or the level of technology, etc.
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to terminate the commission of a specialized research institute, he or she shall clearly notify the specialized research institute and the supervisory organization in charge thereof with the grounds therefor and retrieve the certificate of designation of the specialized research institute.
  (3) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to terminate the commission of specialized research institute pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall hold a hearing.

Article 64 (Revocation of designation of defense materials) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall review whether to maintain or revoke the designation of all defense materials every 3 years and take measures accordingly.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may analyze, for the review under paragraph (1), the domestic technology level in each defense industry sector and the availability of manufacturers capable of producing each defense material, etc. <Added on Mar. 31, 2016>
  (3) Where the Minister of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to revoke the designation of defense materials, he or she shall notify the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources and the relevant defense contractor thereof by specifying the reasons. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Mar. 31, 2016; Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 64-2 (Approval for participation in national strategic arms projects) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall determine and publicly notify types of projects subject to approval referred to in Article 50-2(1) of the Act, among the projects researching and developing strategically valuable arms pursuant to Article 8 of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act. <Amended on Mar. 30, 2021>
  (2) Any enterprise which a foreign enterprise or a foreigner who intends to obtain approval to participate in a national strategic arms project or a project corresponding thereto (hereinafter referred to as "strategic arms project") substantially acquires control over management of (hereinafter referred to as "enterprise subject to approval") pursuant to Article 50-2(1) of the Act shall submit an application for approval prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration with documents referred to in the following subparagraphs attached thereto: <Amended on Jul. 2, 2019>
  1. The articles of association, statement of financial position, and statement of profit and loss for the last three years of a foreign enterprise (hereinafter referred to as "foreign investing enterprise") which has acquired substantial control over the management of an enterprise subject to approval as well as those of the enterprise subject to approval;
  2. Record of mergers and acquisitions of a foreign investing enterprise and an enterprise subject to approval for the last five years;
  3. Management structure and the current status of subsidiaries of a foreign investing enterprise and an enterprise subject to approval;
  4. Record of investments and a plan for investment of a foreign investing enterprise (including subsidiaries) in an enterprise subject to approval;
  5. The current status of exchange of human resources (including dispatch and education) between a foreign investing enterprise (including subsidiaries) and an enterprise subject to approval for the last three years;
  6. Materials referred to in the following items related to security of an enterprise subject to approval:
  a. Security measures for the area where the relevant facilities may be fully protected and for the facilities;
  b. Security measures for the personnel engaged in an enterprise subject to approval;
  c. Security measures for handling, keeping and management of confidential documents;
  d. Measures to protect manufactured goods and raw materials;
  e. Measures to protect equipment and facilities;
  f. Security measures for communications facilities and means of communications;
  g. Measures to protect the process of information processing of various materials and to protect data resulting from information processing;
  h. Means of organic communications with the relevant information agency against any security accident.
  (3) If the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration receives an application for approval pursuant to paragraph (2), he or she shall decide whether he or she approves within 60 days from the date he or she receives such an application and notify the applicant concerned thereof, and when he or she has approved, he or she shall issue an applicant a letter of approval prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  (4) When an enterprise subject to approval submits a proposal to participate in a strategic arms project, it shall submit a letter of approval pursuant to paragraph (3).
  (5) The criteria for the substantial acquisition of control over management pursuant to Article 50-2(2) of the Act shall be any of the following cases:
  1. Where stocks, etc. (including stakes or all the other property rights; hereinafter the same shall apply) of an enterprise are traded, exchanged or merged between enterprises, a security right thereof is exercised, payment in substitutes thereof is received, or are acquired in a lump by other methods;
  2. Where a new corporation is incorporated by separating part of an enterprise therefrom, a separated part is traded, exchanged, or merged between enterprises, a security right thereof is exercised, payment in substitutes thereof is received, or is acquired in whole by other methods;
  3. Where the same person holds not less than 50/100 of the stocks, etc. of an enterprise, alone or jointly with any person (hereinafter referred to as "person who has relation with the same person") under any of the following items (including cases where the same person becomes the largest stockholder, as cases where he or she holds less than 50/100, and becomes able to exercise a dominant influence over the appointment of executives of an enterprise or management thereof for himself or herself or through a person who has relation with the same person):
  a. Spouse, blood relatives as far as third cousins, and relatives as far as cousins of in-laws;
  b. A firm which the same person exercises a dominant influence over the principal decision-making, such as change in organization of the relevant firm or investment in new business, or the management of business for himself or herself or through a person referred to in item a or c;
  c. A firm which the same person may appoint or dismiss the representative director of the relevant firm or appoint not less than 50/100 of the executive officers by contract or agreement with other major stockholders;
  4. Where an enterprise is managed by means of the acquisition by transfer or lease of the whole or important part of business thereof, or acceptance of entrustment of the management thereof.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 1, 2010]

CHAPTER VII SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 64-3 (Approval for appointment of foreigners and persons with multiple nationalities as executive officers of defense contractors) (1) If a defense contractor intends to appoint a foreigner or a person with multiple nationalities (referring to a person with multiple nationalities under Article 11-2(1) of the Nationality Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) as an executive officer or employ such person as an employee handling defense technology (referring to defense technology under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Defense Technology Security Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) under Article 50-3(1) of the Act, the contractor shall submit to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, 90 days before the scheduled date of appointment or employment, an application for approval in the form prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, along with the following documents:
  1. A copy of a passport, certificate of foreign registration, or other identification document by which the nationality of the foreigner or person with multiple nationalities subject to approval can be verified;
  2. A criminal record certificate issued by the home country or the Government of the Republic of Korea for the foreigner or person with multiple nationalities subject to approval, or other equivalent documents.
  (2) Upon receipt of an application for approval under paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall review the following matters in order to determine whether to approve the appointment or employment of the relevant foreigner or person with multiple nationalities under the former part of Article 50-3(2) of the Act; in such cases, if necessary, the Administrator may request cooperation from the heads of relevant administrative agencies, including the National Intelligence Service:
  1. The importance of the defense technology to be handled by the foreigner or person with multiple nationalities subject to approval and its impact on national security;
  2. Matters regarding the identity of the foreigner or person with multiple nationalities subject to approval, including nationality, professional background, and potential ties to foreign intelligence agencies;
  3. The history of violations of security-related statutes or regulations, including technology leakage, by the foreigner or person with multiple nationalities subject to approval (including violations committed in a foreign country);
  4. The results of a fact-finding survey of the relevant defense contractor under Article 12 of the Defense Technology Security Act.
  (3) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may approve an appointment or employment subject to any of the following conditions under the latter part of Article 50-3(2) of the Act:
  1. Restrictions on access to military secrets or defense technology specified by the Minister of National Defense;
  2. Complete separation of the information and communications network used for business by the foreigner or person with multiple nationalities subject to approval from external internet networks;
  3. Other conditions deemed necessary by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration for the protection of defense technology, etc.
  (4) Notification of whether approval is granted under Article 50-3(2) of the Act shall be provided in writing, and if approval is granted, a certificate of approval in the form prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense shall also be issued.
  (5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for the procedures, etc. for approval for the appointment of foreigners and persons with multiple nationalities as executive officers shall be determined and publicly notified by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 8, 2026]

Article 65 (Consultation on manufacture and sales contracts for defense materials) (1) Where a person other than a government agency intends to obtain approval for conclusion of a contract for manufacturing and selling defense materials with a defense contractor in accordance with Article 51 of the Act, he or she shall submit an application for approval on the conclusion of a contract for manufacturing and selling defense materials to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration has received an application for approval pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall determine within three months whether to grant approval in consideration of the requirements of the military, and notify the applicant and defense contractor thereof.

Article 66 (Requirements for permission to manufacture military firearms, swords and explosives) (1) Cases where it is required to obtain permission from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration for military firearms, swords, explosives, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "military firearms, etc.") pursuant to Article 53(1) of the Act shall be as follows; provided, for military firearms, etc. meeting the standards prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, the following acts (limited to subparagraph 9 in cases of military firearms and swords; and excluding subparagraph 5 in cases of military explosives) may be conducted without permission: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020; May 11, 2021; Feb. 11, 2022>
  1. Where a person intends to engage in manufacturing military firearms, etc.;
  2. Where a person intends to add manufacturing items of military firearms, etc.;
  3. Where a person intends to build, expand or change (referring to change of the structure of a factory, etc.; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) manufacturing facilities of military firearms, etc., except in the following cases:
  a. Where the Agency for Defense Development intends to build, expand or change manufacturing facilities in connection with the activities under Article 7(1) of the Act on the Agency for Defense Development;
  b. Where the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality intends to build, expand or change manufacturing facilities in connection with the implementation of the projects under Article 32(6) of the Act, Article 71(2) of this Decree, Article 16(1) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act and Article 19(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  4. Where a person intends to use manufacturing facilities of military firearms, etc. after newly building, expanding, or changing such facilities, except in the following cases:
  a. Where the Agency for Defense Development intends to use such facilities after newly building, expanding, or changing the facilities in connection with performing activities under Article 7(1) of the Act on the Agency for Defense Development;
  b. Where the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality intends to use such facilities after newly building, expanding, or changing the facilities in connection with the implementation of the projects under Article 32(6) of the Act, Article 71(2) of this Decree, Article 16(1) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act and Article 19(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  5. Where a person intends to export or import military firearms, etc.;
  6. Where a person intends to transfer or acquire military firearms, etc., except in the following cases:
  a. Where a person has consulted on and obtained approval for a sales contract of defense materials pursuant to Article 51(2) of the Act;
  b. Where military firearms, etc. are to be delivered to armed forces according to a procurement contract;
  c. Where a person who has obtained import permission under subparagraph 5 intends to transfer to an end user under a contract according to the purpose of the import;
  d. Where each service branch seeks to transfer or acquire;
  7. Where a person intends to carry military firearms, etc. except in the following cases:
  a. Where a person carries military firearms, etc. manufactured by a person who has obtained permission for manufacturing pursuant to subparagraph 1 within manufacturing facilities;
  b. Where a person, who has obtained permission for export or import, permission for transfer or acquisition, permission for storage, permission for transport, or permission for disuse (including cases where transfer, acquisition, storage, transport and disuse are possible without permission) pursuant to subparagraphs 5, 6, and 8 through 10, intends to carry;
  c. Where each service branch intends to carry;
  8. Where a person intends to store military firearms, etc. in a place other than manufacturing facilities permitted for use pursuant to subparagraph 4, except the following cases:
  a. Where the person intends to store in a facility on the premises of the military base, such as a military base ammunition depot;
  b. Where each service branch intends to store;
  9. Where a person intends to transport military firearms, etc., except in the following cases:
  a. Transporting within the area of one business site (multiple business places bordering each other are considered as one business domain) without going through a road under Article 10 of the Road Act;
  b. Transporting military firearms, etc. that are being developed by the Agency for Defense Development (including military firearms, etc. being managed by the defense contractors, etc. as the Agency for Defense Development assigns the production of prototypes, etc. to the defense contractors, etc. for development);
  c. Where the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality intends to transport in connection with the implementation of projects under Article 32(6) of the Act, Article 71(2) of this Decree, Article 16(1) of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act, and Article 19(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act;
  d. Where each service branch intends to transport;
  10. Where a person intends to disuse military firearms, etc. except in the following cases:
  a. Where the person intends to disuse military firearms, etc. wherein defects are detected in the manufacturing process in the manufacturing facilities permitted for pursuant to subparagraph 4 (including cases where using manufacturing facilities is possible without permission);
  b. Where each service branch intends to disuse.
  (2) A person who intends to obtain permission pursuant to paragraph (1) shall apply for permission to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. In such cases, where a person intends to obtain permission to use manufacturing facilities of military firearms, etc. pursuant to paragraph (1)4, he or she shall file an application within 20 days from the completion date of the construction, expansion or change of manufacturing facilities. <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020>
  (3) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration grants permission (only applicable to permission falling under paragraph (1)1 through 4 and 8) upon application for permission under paragraph (2), he or she shall determine whether to grant such permission in consideration of the following requirements. In such cases, he or she shall require the president of the Agency for Defense Development to conduct a safety inspection of the manufacturing facilities and storage facilities of military firearms, etc. (hereafter in this paragraph referred to as "manufacturing facilities") and shall reflect the result therein:
  1. To secure a safety distance between manufacturing facilities, and to the adjacent residential area;
  2. To secure safety in the structure of manufacturing facilities and their incidental facilities;
  3. To secure safety in protection of manufacturing facilities;
  4. To secure maintenance of appropriate supply and quality assurance according to the demand of armed forces.
  (4) Detailed matters concerning procedures for permission of military firearms, etc. under paragraph (1) and requirements for permission, etc. referred to in paragraph (3) shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  (5) In cases of a national emergency, or where security is deemed necessary, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may request the commander of a military unit designated by the Minister of National Defense to escort the transport of military firearms, etc.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jul. 1, 2009]

Article 66-2 (Safety management of military firearms) (1) A person who has obtained permission pursuant to Article 66(1)4 (hereinafter referred to as "person who has obtained permission to use manufacturing facilities") and a person who has obtained permission pursuant to subparagraph 8 of that paragraph (hereinafter referred to as "person who has obtained permission for the storage") shall establish hazard prevention regulations as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, which shall be complied with by themselves and their employees.
  (2) A person who has obtained permission to use manufacturing facilities or a person who has obtained permission for the storage shall establish his or her own plan for safety education for the relevant employees, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, and shall faithfully conduct safety education accordingly.
  (3) A person who has obtained permission to use manufacturing facilities or a person who has obtained permission for the storage shall establish and implement a safety inspection plan including regular inspections of at least twice a year, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, and shall report the inspection results to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration without delay after completing a regular inspection.
  (4) A person who has obtained permission to use manufacturing facilities or a person who has obtained permission for the storage shall undergo a safety inspection conducted by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 31, 2020]

Article 66-3 (Appointment of persons in charge of security in manufacturing and managing military explosives) (1) A person who has obtained permission to use manufacturing facilities for military explosives shall appoint a person in charge of security in manufacturing military explosives and a person in charge of security in managing military explosives; and a person who has obtained permission for the storage of military explosives shall appoint a person in charge of security in managing military explosives, respectively, from among persons who have obtained a license prescribed in Article 28 of the Act on the Safety Management of Guns, Swords, and Explosives.
  (2) Where a person who is required to appoint a person in charge of security in manufacturing military explosives or a person in charge of security in managing military explosives pursuant to paragraph (1) appoints or dismisses a person in charge of security in manufacturing military explosives or a person in charge of security in managing military explosives, he or she shall report to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  (3) A person in charge of security in manufacturing military explosives shall supervise matters concerning the manufacturing process of military explosives and a person in charge of security in managing military explosives shall supervise overall affairs of handling (excluding manufacturing) military explosives, respectively; provided, they shall faithfully perform supervisory duties for safety including the following matters:
  1. To ensure that manufacturing facilities, etc. are maintained in conformity with the standards as at the time of permission;
  2. To guide and supervise the hazard prevention regulations and the status of compliance under Article 66-2(1);
  3. To guide and supervise a safety education plan and the status of implementation thereof under Article 66-2(2);
  4. To guide and supervise the safety inspection plan and the status of implementation thereof under Article 66-2(3).
  (4) A person handling military explosives shall follow the safety instruction and supervision by a person in charge of security in manufacturing military explosives and a person in charge of security in managing military explosives.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 31, 2020]

Article 67 (Reserve of raw materials) (1) The kind and quantity of raw materials that the defense contractor must reserve pursuant to Article 55 of the Act and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may notify the relevant defense contractors of the details of raw materials that they must reserve pursuant to paragraph (1), and order them to reserve raw materials accordingly.
  (3) A defense contractor, in receipt of the order referred to in paragraph (2), shall reserve raw materials within one year from the date it is ordered to do so, and if it fails to reserve raw materials within the prescribed period, it shall notify the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration of the reason and the expected date of reserving.
  (4) When a defense contractor who has received an order under paragraph (2) intends to import or purchase raw materials to be reserved, it shall undergo an inspection for standards and quality from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Added on May 11, 2021>

Article 68 (Permission for exportation) (1) A person, who intends to engage in exportation or intermediation business pursuant to Article 57(1) of the Act, shall submit a report on exportation or intermediation business to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, along with the documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010>
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, in receipt of a report submitted under paragraph (1), shall issue a certificate of reporting exportation or intermediation business to the relevant reporting person. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2010>
  (3) A person, who intends to obtain permission for exporting defense materials and defense science and technology or intermediating the trade thereof pursuant to the main clause of Article 57(2) of the Act, shall submit a written application to obtain permission for exportation or trade intermediation prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration, along with the documents prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. ＜ Amended on Sep. 22, 2015; Jun. 20, 2017>
  (4) "Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where defense materials and defense science and technology are exported abroad for provision to the Republic of Korea troops dispatched abroad" in the proviso of Article 57(2) of the Act means any of the following cases; provided, any of the cases specified in subparagraphs 4 through 6, which requires the consent or permission of a foreign government for export, or any of the cases specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (7), shall be excluded herefrom: <Added on Sep. 22, 2015; Jun. 20, 2017; Mar. 31, 2020; Jun. 30, 2026>
  1. Where the major defense materials and defense science and technology are supplied for the use of the national army dispatched overseas;
  2. Where the major defense materials and defense science and technology are supplied for the use of embassies and legations abroad;
  3. Where the major defense materials and defense science and technology are supplied for the emergency repair use for the security of fleet or military aircraft of Korea;
  4. Where goods identical to the defense materials exported with permission from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration under the main clause of Article 57(2) of the Act (excluding repair parts) are exported, within 2 years from the date such permission was granted, to the same end user (limited to cases where the end user is the government of the importing country); in such cases, the Administrator shall determine and publicly notify detailed criteria for recognizing goods as identical, including goods whose names and specifications are identical to those of the defense materials exported;
  5. Where repair parts for the defense materials exported with permission from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration under the main clause of Article 57(2) of the Act are exported, within 5 years from the date such permission was granted, to the same end user (limited to cases where the end user is the government of the importing country); in such cases, the Administrator shall determine and publicly notify detailed criteria for recognizing goods as repair parts, including intended for the performance of warranty repairs;
  6. Where defense materials, which have been imported for repair or replacement for reasons such as poor performance, defect, or damage after exporting with permission from the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration pursuant to the main clause of Article 57(2) of the Act, are subsequently exported to the same end users.
  (5) The scope of defense materials and national defense science and technology permitted to be exported pursuant to Article 57 of the Act shall be prescribed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration. <Amended on Jun. 30, 2026>
  (6) Where the guarantee of the performance of contracts and of quality is requested from the government of the purchasing country when exporting defense materials and national defense science and technology pursuant to paragraph (5), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may comply therewith. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 22, 2015; Jun. 30, 2026>
  (7) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may restrict exportation or order coordination of major defense materials and national defense science and technology referred to in Article 34(1) of the Act, pursuant to Article 57(4) of the Act, in each of the following cases: <Amended on Jul. 1, 2009; Oct. 1, 2010; Sep. 22, 2015; Mar. 31, 2020; Mar. 30, 2021; Jun. 30, 2026>
  1. Where it is necessary for international peace, maintenance of safety, and national security, or where there are urgent changes in international situations, such as war and terrorism;
  2. Where diplomatic friction is likely to occur, as a result of exporting major defense materials and defense science and technology;
  3. Where it is necessary to comply with agreements concluded between governments regarding an agreement for importation of technology with a foreign country or export control of strategic materials;
  4. Where it is apprehended that national interests might be damaged due to excessive competition between domestic enterprises that export major defense materials and defense science and technology;
  5. Where items, the quality of which has not been guaranteed, are exported, or where disqualified items are exported;
  6. Where it is apprehended that assistance in subsequent supplies of war following the exportation of defense materials might be hindered;
  7. Where a person breaches a contract for technology transfer referred to in Article 16(4) of the Enforcement Decree of the Defense Science and Technology Innovation Promotion Act.
  (8) Deleted. <Jun. 20, 2017>
  [Title Amended on Sep. 22, 2015]

Article 68-2 (Registration of munitions sales agency) (1) Any person who intends to register a military supply sales agency business pursuant to Article 57-2(1) of the Act shall submit an application for registration of a military supply sales agency business (including an application for registration in an electronic form) prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration along with the following documents (including electronic documents): <Amended on Dec. 3, 2019>
  1. Resumes of its representative and executive officers;
  2. Current status of employment, such as the total number of employees and the number of employees related to defense acquisition programs;
  3. A pledge of integrity under Article 6(1) of the Act;
  4. Deleted. <Dec. 3, 2019>
  (2) Upon receipt of an application under paragraph (1), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall verify the following documents through administrative data matching under Article 36(1) of the Electronic Government Act; provided, where the applicant does not consent to verification, the Minister shall require the applicant to submit the relevant documents: <Amended on Apr. 30, 2024>
  1. In cases of a corporation, a corporation registration certificate;
  2. Business registration certificate (referring to business registration certificate excluding resident registration numbers).
  (3) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration receives an application for registration of military supply sales agency business pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall issue a registration certificate to an applicant within 30 days from the date he or she receives the application after checking whether matters in the application fall under any subparagraph of Article 57-2(1) of the Act, and the relevant facts.
  (4) Where it is necessary to verify the relevant facts under paragraph (3), the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may extend the period required for issuance of a registration certificate within 15 days, and notify an applicant of reasons for the extension thereof and the extended period.
  [This Article Added on Nov. 29, 2016]

Article 68-3 (Registration of alterations of munitions sales agency) (1) "Important matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 57-2(2) of the Act means the following:
  1. Representative and executive officers;
  2. Company (corporation) name;
  3. Location of a place of business and contact details.
  (2) Where a person intends to register any alterations of military supply sales agency business pursuant to Article 57-2(2) of the Act, he or she shall submit an application for registration of alterations of military supply sales agency business (including an application for registration of alterations in an electronic form) prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense within 30 days from the date such alterations are made to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration along with the following:
  1. Documents evidencing the relevant amendment;
  2. A certificate of registration (only applicable where a matter included in the certificate of registration is modified).
  (3) The provisions of Article 68-2(2) through (4) shall apply mutatis mutandis to procedures for examining documents relating to registration of alterations and issuing a registration certificate.
  (4) Where a military supply sales agent intends to continuously conduct military supply sales agency business even after the effective period of registration under Article 57-2(4) of the Act, he or she may file an application for renewal of registration with the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration from three months before the expiration date of the effective period to one month before the expiration date thereof. In such cases, Article 68-2 shall apply mutatis mutandis to procedures for applying for renewal of registration and the issuance of a registration certificate.
  (5) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall prenotify the relevant military supply sales agent of procedures for renewal of registration and the fact that the effective period will expire unless he or she applies for renewal within the period by text messaging, email, fax, telephone, etc. no later than three months prior to the expiration date of the effective period of registration.
  [This Article Added on Nov. 29, 2016]

Article 68-4 (Hearings) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to revoke registration of military supply sales agency business pursuant to Article 57-3(1) of the Act, he or she shall hold a hearing.
  [This Article Added on Nov. 29, 2016]

Article 68-5 (Reporting of commission) (1) "Project of at least the size prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 57-4(1) of the Act means a project whose budget for a contract which the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to enter into is at least two million US dollars.
  (2) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration invites competitive tenders for a project (hereinafter referred to as "project subject to reporting of commission") in which case he or she should report remuneration, such as commission, (hereinafter referred to as "commission") under Article 57-4(1) of the Act, he or she shall specify that the project is subject to reporting of commission in an invitation to tender, and where he or she enters into a private contract, he or she shall notify the other party to a contract or a military supply sales agent who intermediates or represents the relevant contract of the fact that the project is subject to reporting of commission before he or she enters into the contract.
  (3) Any person who intends to report commission pursuant to Article 57-4(1) of the Act shall submit a report mentioning a party to the contract concerning commission, the commission, etc. to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration by the deadline for submitting a tender (in cases of a contract concluded through negotiations prescribed in Article 43 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party, referring to a proposal) in cases of competitive tenders, and by the date he or she enters into the contract in cases of a private contract; provided, where he or she enters into a contract concerning commission after the deadline for submitting a tender or proposal or the date he or she enters into a private contract, he or she shall submit the report to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration within 30 days after he or she enters into a contract for the relevant commission.
  (4) Any person who intends to alter matters reported pursuant to Article 57-4(2) of the Act shall submit a report on alterations to the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration within 30 days after he or she alters such matters.
  (5) Where the Commissioner of the National Tax Service conducts a tax investigation under the Framework Act on National Taxes (including a tax offense investigation under the Procedure for the Punishment of Tax Offenses Act) in regard to taxation data related to commission reports submitted by a military supply sales agent pursuant to Article 3 and the Appendix of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Submission and Management of Taxation Data, if any investigation results are found to be different from the taxation data, the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall be notified thereof. <Added on Dec. 3, 2019>
  (6) Where the Commissioner of the Korea Customs Service conducts an investigation under Article 110(2)2 or Article 290 of the Customs Act on taxation data related to commission reports of a military supply sales agent pursuant to Article 263-2(1) and Appendix 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Customs Act, if any investigation results differ from the taxation data, he or she shall notify the Commissioner of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration thereof. <Added on Dec. 3, 2019>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 20, 2017]

Article 69 (Recovery of fraudulent gains) (1) Where the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration intends to recover fraudulent gains and additional levies (hereinafter referred to as "fraudulent gains, etc.") pursuant to Article 58(1) of the Act, he or she shall serve a written notification for payment by clearly stating the fact of fraudulent gains, amount of fraudulent gains, etc., deadline for payment, and the methods of and period for, etc. raising objections. <Amended on Jun. 20, 2017>
  (2) A person who has been notified pursuant to paragraph (1) shall pay the fraudulent gains, etc. to an organization appointed by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration within 30 days from the date of such notification.
  (3) The basis and methods for calculation of additional charges prescribed in Article 58 of the Act shall be as specified in the Appendix 1. <Added on Jun. 20, 2017>

Article 70 (Restrictions on qualifications for participation in bidding) (1) Where a person falling under Article 6(1)4 of the Act falls under any subparagraph of Article 59(1) of the Act, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall restrict the qualification of a person falling under any subparagraph of Article 6(1)4 of the Act to participate in a tendering procedure for at least one month but not more than five years. If deemed necessary, the Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may undergo deliberation by the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee, taking into consideration the type of restricted acts, whether such restricted acts are intentionally or negligently committed, the degree of damage to the State, etc.
  (2) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall also prohibit the representative or executive officer of an enterprise or research institute subject to restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering pursuant to paragraph (1) from participating in tendering; provided, this shall not apply where the representative or executive officer of another enterprise or research institute has several representatives or executive officers and the representative or executive officer of the enterprise or research institute subject to restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering pursuant to paragraph (1) fails to participate in tendering. <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024>
  (3) Article 76(11) through (14) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall apply mutatis mutandis to the disclosure, etc. of restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering under Article 59(5) of the Act. <Amended on Sep. 20, 2024>
  (4) In cases falling under any subparagraph of Article 59(1) of the Act, the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party shall apply to matters not prescribed in this Decree concerning restrictions on qualifications for participation in tendering procedures.
  (5) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (4), matters necessary for detailed criteria for restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering shall be prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 70-2 (Legal fiction as public officials) Members of the Committee prescribed by Presidential Decree in Article 60(1)3 of the Act means members of the Defense Acquisition Contract Deliberation Committee (including members of subcommittees). <Amended on Jul. 16, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 30, 2024]

Article 71 (Entrustment of duties) (1) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall, under Article 61(2) of the Act, entrust the following tasks to the President of the Agency for Defense Development: <Amended on Jul. 1, 2009; Dec. 17, 2013; Mar. 31, 2020; Mar. 30, 2021; Feb. 11, 2022>
  1. Deleted; <Dec. 23, 2025>
  1-2. Deleted; <Mar. 30, 2021>
  2. Deleted; <Mar. 30, 2021>
  3. Safety inspections on manufacturing facilities and storage facilities of military firearms, etc. prescribed in the latter part of Article 66(3);
  4. Safety inspections of persons who have obtained permission to use manufacturing facilities of military firearms, etc. and persons who have obtained permission for the storage of military firearms, etc. pursuant to Article 66-2(4).
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration shall, under Article 61(2) of the Act, entrust the following tasks to the President of the Defense Agency for Technology and Quality: <Amended on Jul. 1, 2009; Nov. 4, 2014; Mar. 31, 2016; Jul. 19, 2016; Jun. 20, 2017; Sep. 22, 2017; May 28, 2018; Mar. 31, 2020; Feb. 2, 2021; Mar. 30, 2021; May 11, 2021; Feb. 11, 2022; Aug. 16, 2023; Dec. 23, 2025>
  1. Contracts for, and management of, projects determined by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration to improve the performance of weapons systems under Article 22(1) of the Act;
  2. Verification, coordination, and control of configuration details regarding design errors, modifications, etc. that do not affect the required operational capability of military supplies, the schedule for fielding, etc., or costs at the mass production and operation and maintenance phases, among the tasks related to verification, coordination, and control of configuration details under Article 26(3) of the Act and Article 32(1)3 of this Decree;
  3. The following tasks among the tasks regarding quality assurance under Article 28 of the Act:
  a. Quality assurance, including inspections for completed work, at the research and development, mass production, purchase (limited to domestic purchase), and operation and maintenance phases of naval weapons systems, such as combat ships;
  b. Quality assurance, including the issuance of inspection reports for weapons systems in which expeditious requirements are determined under Article 15-2(1) of the Act or for weapons systems which have undergone a pilot project under Article 17-2(1) of the Act;
  c. Quality assurance, including the issuance of inspection reports for weapons7 systems at the research and development (limited to projects determined by the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration as projects for fielding prototypes), mass production, and operation and maintenance phases, and for force support systems designated by the Administrator;
  d. Deleted; <Dec. 23, 2025>
  e. Deleted; <Dec. 23, 2025>
  f. Conclusion of international quality assurance agreements with foreign governments to promote international cooperation on quality assurance;
  g. Management for preventing counterfeit parts in the process of quality assurance, and investigation, analysis, and information management on counterfeit parts;
  4. Receipt, examination, renewal and examination of the post management of an application for certification of the quality management system prescribed in Article 29-2 of the Act;
  5. Deleted; <Feb. 2, 2021>
  6. Supervision of the transportation and disuse of military firearms, etc. under Article 53(1) of the Act;
  7. Verification of the status of management of defense materials under Article 55(6);
  7-2. Analysis of the domestic technology level in each defense industry sector and the availability of manufacturers capable of producing each defense material, etc. under Article 64(2);
  8. Verification of types and quantities and inspection of standards and quality of the raw materials to be reserved under Article 67.
  [Title Amended on Feb. 11, 2022]

Article 71-2 (Management of sensitive information and personally identifiable information) (1) The Minister of National Defense or the Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may manage information constituting criminal history records under subparagraph 2 of Article 18 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act (limited to affairs under subparagraph 2) or data containing resident registration numbers under subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the same Decree or alien registration numbers under subparagraph 4 of the same Article, if it is inevitable to perform the following affairs: <Added on Jul. 16, 2024>
  1. Request for inquiry into criminal records under Article 6-2 of the Act;
  2. Affairs concerning restrictions on qualification for participation in tendering procedures under Article 59 of the Act.
  (2) The Administrator of the Defense Acquisition Program Administration may manage information corresponding to criminal history records under subparagraph 2 of Article 18 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act or data containing resident registration numbers under subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of that Decree, passport numbers under subparagraph 2 of that Article (limited to the business affairs specified in subparagraph 1), or foreigner registration numbers under subparagraph 4 of that Article, if it is unavoidable to perform the following business affairs: <Amended on Mar. 31, 2020; Jul. 16, 2024; Sep. 8, 2026>
  1. Approval for the appointment of foreigners and persons with multiple nationalities as executive officers under Article 50-3 of the Act;
  2. Registration of military supply sales agency business referred to in Article 57-2 of the Act;
  3. Revocation of the registration of military supply sales agency business under Article 57-3 of the Act.
  (3) The Guarantee institution designated pursuant to Article 43(1) of the Act may manage data including resident registration numbers under subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act or alien registration numbers under subparagraph 4 of the same Article, if it is inevitable to perform guarantee business under the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) of that Article. <Added on Mar. 31, 2020; Jul. 16, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 3, 2019]

Article 72 (Criteria for imposing administrative fines) The criteria for the imposition of administrative fines under Article 64(1) of the Act shall be as prescribed in Appendix 2.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 20, 2017]

